window.QUIZ={"course":{"code":"MATH204","key":"math204.quiz.v1","prof":"★ past quiz / exam question","sub":"A–D and True/False · one chapter per topic · progress saved in this browser","exams":[{"name":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","when":"Week 5 (~26–30 Oct 2026)","scope":"3 written questions, 50 min: restating (negation, contrapositive…) · arguments/equivalence · direct proof & counterexample — NO divisibility"},{"name":"Midterm","date":"2026-11-23","when":"TBA (week 8–9)","scope":"Epp ch. 1–4"}]},"built":"2026-10-26","chapters":[{"id":"m02","title":"Logic of Compound Statements (§2.1–2.2)","short":"Compound stmts","exam":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","w":1.3,"topics":["Connectives & translation","Only if, necessary, sufficient","Truth tables","Equivalence laws","De Morgan & negation","Conditional, converse, inverse, contrapositive","Tautology & contradiction"],"n":43},{"id":"m03","title":"Valid & Invalid Arguments (§2.3)","short":"Arguments","exam":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","w":1.3,"topics":["Validity & critical rows","Modus ponens & tollens","Rules of inference","Fallacies","Validity vs truth & soundness","Deduction chains"],"n":34},{"id":"m04","title":"Quantified Statements (§3.1–3.4)","short":"Quantifiers","exam":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","w":0.7,"topics":["Truth of ∀ and ∃","Negating quantifiers","Nested quantifiers","Conditional variants","Necessary, sufficient, only if","Arguments with quantifiers"],"n":35},{"id":"m05","title":"Methods of Proof I (§4.1–4.3)","short":"Proof I","exam":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","w":1.2,"topics":["Even, odd, prime","Rational numbers","Existence and counterexample","Direct proof","Common mistakes","Divisibility proofs"],"n":30},{"id":"m01","title":"Speaking Mathematically (ch. 1, Week 1)","short":"Speaking math","exam":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","w":0.5,"topics":["Statements & variables","Statement types","Sets & subsets","Tuples & Cartesian products","Relations & functions","Graphs & pigeonhole"],"n":21},{"id":"m06","title":"Methods of Proof II (§4.4–4.7)","short":"Proof II","exam":"Midterm","date":"2026-11-23","w":0.7,"topics":["Divisibility","Quotient-remainder, div & mod","Floor and ceiling","Proof by cases","Contradiction","Contraposition"],"n":25}],"questions":[{"id":"m02-001","chapter":"m02","topic":"Connectives & translation","type":"mcq","q":"h = \"it is hot\", s = \"it is sunny\". Translate: \"It is not hot but it is sunny.\"","options":["¬(h ∧ s)","¬h ∨ s","¬h ∧ s","¬h → s"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"In logic \"but\" means \"and\", and the \"not\" attaches only to hot. ¬(h ∧ s) would negate the whole conjunction (\"not both hot and sunny\"), which is a different statement.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 13 · Epp p. 40 (Example 2.1.3)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §5 Translating English","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-002","chapter":"m02","topic":"Connectives & translation","type":"mcq","q":"h = \"it is hot\", s = \"it is sunny\". Translate: \"It is neither hot nor sunny.\"","options":["¬(h ∧ s)","¬h ∨ ¬s","¬h ∧ s","¬h ∧ ¬s"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"\"Neither p nor q\" means ¬p ∧ ¬q (≡ ¬(p ∨ q)). ¬(h ∧ s) and ¬h ∨ ¬s only say it is not both, which is true on a hot cloudy day.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 13 · Epp p. 40 (Example 2.1.3)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §5 Translating English","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-003","chapter":"m02","topic":"Connectives & translation","type":"mcq","q":"x is a fixed real number; p = \"x > 0\", q = \"x < 3\", r = \"x = 3\". Which form expresses 0 < x ≤ 3?","options":["p ∧ (q ∨ r)","(p ∧ q) ∨ r","p ∨ (q ∧ r)","p ∧ q ∧ r"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"x ≤ 3 is q ∨ r, and 0 < x ≤ 3 is p ∧ (x ≤ 3). The parentheses are required: (p ∧ q) ∨ r is a different form, since ∧ and ∨ are coequal.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 14 · Epp p. 42 (Example 2.1.4)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §5 Translating English","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-004","chapter":"m02","topic":"Connectives & translation","type":"tf","q":"By Epp's order of operations, p ∧ q ∨ r has one definite meaning, because ∧ is performed before ∨.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"∧ and ∨ are coequal, so p ∧ q ∨ r is ambiguous: (p ∧ q) ∨ r and p ∧ (q ∨ r) differ when p is F and r is T. Only ¬ is performed first.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 12 · Epp p. 40","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §4 Order of operations","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-005","chapter":"m02","topic":"Connectives & translation","type":"mcq","q":"p = \"the night hunting is successful\", q = \"the moon is full\", r = \"the sky is cloudless\". Translate: \"If the moon is full but the sky is not cloudless, then the night hunting is not successful.\"","options":["¬p → (q ∧ ¬r)","(q ∧ ¬r) → ¬p","(q ∨ ¬r) → ¬p","q ∧ (¬r → ¬p)"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"\"But\" is ∧, so the hypothesis is q ∧ ¬r and the conclusion ¬p. Writing ¬p → (q ∧ ¬r) reverses the conditional (its converse).","source":"2025 Sample Quiz Q1a · Epp p. 40","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §3 Sample Quiz Q1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-006","chapter":"m02","topic":"Connectives & translation","type":"mcq","q":"Same p, q, r (hunting successful; moon full; sky cloudless). Translate: \"For successful night hunting, it is necessary that the moon is full and the sky is cloudless.\"","options":["(q ∧ r) → p","p ↔ (q ∧ r)","p → (q ∧ r)","¬p → ¬(q ∧ r)"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"\"A is necessary for B\" means B → A, so successful hunting implies q ∧ r. (q ∧ r) → p would make the conditions sufficient; ¬p → ¬(q ∧ r) is the inverse of the correct form, which is equivalent to that same wrong converse.","source":"2025 Sample Quiz Q1b · Sample Midterm Q1b · Epp p. 61","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §3 Sample Quiz Q1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-007","chapter":"m02","topic":"Only if, necessary, sufficient","type":"mcq","q":"Same p, q, r (hunting successful; moon full; sky cloudless). Translate: \"The sky being cloudy is sufficient for the night hunting to be successful.\"","options":["p → ¬r","r → p","¬r ∧ p","¬r → p"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Cloudy is ¬r (not cloudless), and \"A is sufficient for B\" means A → B. p → ¬r is the necessary-condition reading; r → p forgets that cloudy means not cloudless.","source":"2025 Sample Midterm Q1a · Epp p. 61","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm Q1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-008","chapter":"m02","topic":"Truth tables","type":"mcq","q":"Truth column of the exclusive or (p ∨ q) ∧ ¬(p ∧ q) for the rows (p, q) = TT, TF, FT, FF:","options":["F, T, T, F","T, T, T, F","F, T, T, T","T, F, F, T"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Exclusive or is true when exactly one of p, q is true. T, T, T, F is inclusive or (p ∨ q); T, F, F, T is the biconditional.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 16 · Epp p. 43 (Example 2.1.5)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §6 Truth tables (exclusive or)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-009","chapter":"m02","topic":"Truth tables","type":"mcq","q":"Truth column of p ∨ ¬q → ¬p for the rows (p, q) = TT, TF, FT, FF:","options":["T, F, T, T","F, F, T, T","F, T, F, T","T, T, F, F"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"→ is performed last, so this is (p ∨ ¬q) → ¬p. When p is T the conclusion ¬p is F and the hypothesis is T: rows 1–2 are F. When p is F the conclusion is T: rows 3–4 are T.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 34 · Epp p. 54 (Example 2.2.1)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §14 Conditional statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-010","chapter":"m02","topic":"Truth tables","type":"mcq","q":"For which truth values is p → q false?","options":["p false, q true","p false, q false","p true, q false","p true, q true"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"A conditional is false only when the hypothesis is true and the conclusion false. With p false it is vacuously true, whatever q is.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 32 · Epp p. 54","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §14 Conditional statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-011","chapter":"m02","topic":"Truth tables","type":"tf","q":"The statement \"If 0 = 1, then 1 = 2\" is true.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Its hypothesis 0 = 1 is false, so the conditional is vacuously true. Many students mark it false because the conclusion is false, but p → q is false only for T → F.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 33 · Epp p. 54","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §14 Conditional statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-012","chapter":"m02","topic":"Truth tables","type":"mcq","q":"In how many of the 8 rows of its truth table is p ∧ ¬r ↔ q ∨ r true?","options":["3","1","4","2"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Parse as (p ∧ ¬r) ↔ (q ∨ r); it is true where both sides agree. p ∧ ¬r is T only at TTF and TFF; q ∨ r is F only at TFF and FFF. They agree at TTF (both T) and FFF (both F): 2 rows. TFF is the near miss (T vs F).","source":"Epp Exercise Set 2.2 #9, p. 63","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §20 The biconditional","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-013","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"In the chain ¬(p ∨ ¬q) ∨ (¬p ∧ ¬q) ≡ ¬p, which law justifies the step\n(¬p ∧ q) ∨ (¬p ∧ ¬q) ≡ ¬p ∧ (q ∨ ¬q)?","options":["Distributive law","Absorption law","Associative law","De Morgan's law"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"¬p is factored out of two ∧-terms joined by ∨: that is p ∧ (q ∨ r) ≡ (p ∧ q) ∨ (p ∧ r) read right to left. De Morgan was used in the earlier step that removed ¬( … ∨ … ).","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 1 Q2 [official key] · Epp Thm 2.1.1, p. 49","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §1 Quiz I Section 1, Q2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-014","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"Same chain, final step: ¬p ∧ t ≡ ¬p. Which law is this?","options":["Universal bound law","Identity law","Negation law","Idempotent law"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Identity: p ∧ t ≡ p (t with ∧ leaves p alone). Universal bound is the opposite pairing, p ∧ c ≡ c and p ∨ t ≡ t; the negation law produced the t in the previous step (q ∨ ¬q ≡ t).","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 1 Q2 [official key] · Epp Thm 2.1.1, p. 49","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §1 Quiz I Section 1, Q2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-015","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"Simplify ¬((¬p ∧ q) ∨ (¬p ∧ ¬q)) ∨ (p ∧ q).","options":["¬p","p ∧ q","p","t"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Distributive → ¬(¬p ∧ (q ∨ ¬q)) ∨ (p ∧ q); negation → ¬(¬p ∧ t) ∨ (p ∧ q); identity → ¬(¬p) ∨ (p ∧ q); double negative → p ∨ (p ∧ q); absorption → p. This exact expression appeared on three 2025 papers.","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 2 Q2 [official key] · Sample Quiz Q2 · Sample Midterm Q3","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §2 Quiz I Section 2, Q2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-016","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"In that chain the last step is p ∨ (p ∧ q) ≡ p. Name the law.","options":["Idempotent law","Distributive law","Identity law","Absorption law"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Absorption: p ∨ (p ∧ q) ≡ p and p ∧ (p ∨ q) ≡ p. Idempotent would be p ∨ p ≡ p, with no q involved.","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 2 Q2 [official key] · Epp Thm 2.1.1, p. 49","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §2 Quiz I Section 2, Q2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-017","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"Same chain: ¬(¬p ∧ t) ∨ (p ∧ q) ≡ ¬(¬p) ∨ (p ∧ q). Which law is used?","options":["Identity law","Double negative law","Negation law","Universal bound law"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Inside the brackets ¬p ∧ t ≡ ¬p by the identity law; the outer ¬ is untouched. The double negative law is the next step, turning ¬(¬p) into p.","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 2 Q2 [official key] · Epp Thm 2.1.1, p. 49","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §2 Quiz I Section 2, Q2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-018","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"Simplify ¬(¬p ∧ q) ∧ (p ∨ q).","options":["q","p","p ∧ q","¬q"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"De Morgan + double negative → (p ∨ ¬q) ∧ (p ∨ q); distributive → p ∨ (¬q ∧ q); negation → p ∨ c; identity → p. The result does not depend on q at all.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 28 · Epp p. 50 (Example 2.1.14)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §13 Simplification exercises","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-019","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"p ∨ t ≡ t is an instance of which law?","options":["Identity law","Negation law","Universal bound law","Absorption law"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Universal bound: p ∨ t ≡ t and p ∧ c ≡ c (the bound swallows p). The identity laws are p ∧ t ≡ p and p ∨ c ≡ p.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 27 · Epp Thm 2.1.1, p. 49","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §12 Theorem 2.1.1","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-020","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"With p ⊕ q ≡ (p ∨ q) ∧ ¬(p ∧ q), simplify (p ⊕ p) ⊕ p.","options":["c","t","¬p","p"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"p ⊕ p ≡ p ∧ ¬p ≡ c (idempotent twice, negation), and c ⊕ p ≡ (c ∨ p) ∧ ¬(c ∧ p) ≡ p ∧ t ≡ p. The slide's handwriting stops at c ⊕ p; answering c is the trap.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 29 · Epp Exercise 2.1.46, p. 52","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §13 Simplification exercises (⚠️ slide stops at c ⊕ p)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-021","chapter":"m02","topic":"De Morgan & negation","type":"mcq","q":"Which pair is NOT logically equivalent?","options":["¬(p ∧ q) and ¬p ∧ ¬q","¬(p ∨ q) and ¬p ∧ ¬q","p → q and ¬p ∨ q","p ∧ (p ∨ q) and p"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The rows TF and FT differ: ¬(p ∧ q) is T there, ¬p ∧ ¬q is F. The correct De Morgan form is ¬p ∨ ¬q. The other three pairs are De Morgan, if-then-as-or, and absorption.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 18 · Epp p. 45 (Example 2.1.7)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §7 Logical equivalence","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-022","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"mcq","q":"By the distributive law, p ∨ (q ∧ r) ≡","options":["(p ∨ q) ∨ (p ∨ r)","(p ∨ q) ∧ (p ∨ r)","(p ∧ q) ∨ (p ∧ r)","(p ∨ q) ∧ r"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"∨ distributes over ∧: p ∨ (q ∧ r) ≡ (p ∨ q) ∧ (p ∨ r). (p ∧ q) ∨ (p ∧ r) is the other distributive law's right side, which equals p ∧ (q ∨ r).","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 27 · Epp Thm 2.1.1, p. 49","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §12 Theorem 2.1.1","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-023","chapter":"m02","topic":"De Morgan & negation","type":"mcq","q":"Negate: \"The connector is loose or the machine is unplugged.\"","options":["The connector is not loose or the machine is not unplugged.","The connector is not loose or the machine is unplugged.","The connector is not loose and the machine is not unplugged.","If the connector is loose, then the machine is not unplugged."],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"¬(p ∨ q) ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬q: negate both parts and switch or to and. Keeping \"or\" is the classic slip.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 22 · Epp Exercise 2.1.27, p. 51","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §9 Applying De Morgan's laws","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-024","chapter":"m02","topic":"De Morgan & negation","type":"mcq","q":"Negate: \"John is 6 feet tall and he weighs at least 200 pounds.\"","options":["John is not 6 feet tall and he weighs less than 200 pounds.","John is not 6 feet tall or he weighs at most 200 pounds.","John is not 6 feet tall and he weighs at most 200 pounds.","John is not 6 feet tall or he weighs less than 200 pounds."],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"¬(p ∧ q) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬q, and the negation of \"at least 200\" is \"less than 200\". \"At most 200\" still allows exactly 200, which is not a negation.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 22 · Epp p. 46 (Example 2.1.9)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §9 Applying De Morgan's laws","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-025","chapter":"m02","topic":"De Morgan & negation","type":"mcq","q":"Negate −1 < x ≤ 4 (x a fixed real number).","options":["x ≤ −1 or x > 4","−1 ≥ x > 4","x < −1 or x ≥ 4","x ≤ −1 and x > 4"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"−1 < x ≤ 4 is (x > −1) ∧ (x ≤ 4); De Morgan gives x ≤ −1 ∨ x > 4. \"−1 ≥ x > 4\" is an and-statement that no x satisfies; the third option gets both endpoints wrong.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 23 · Epp p. 47 (Example 2.1.10)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §9 Applying De Morgan's laws (Epp's caution)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-026","chapter":"m02","topic":"De Morgan & negation","type":"mcq","q":"Negate \"x < 2 or x > 5\" (x a fixed real number).","options":["x ≥ 2 or x ≤ 5","2 ≤ x ≤ 5","2 < x < 5","x ≤ 2 and x ≥ 5"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Negate each part (x ≥ 2, x ≤ 5) and switch or to and: x ≥ 2 and x ≤ 5. Keeping \"or\" gives a statement true of every real number; 2 < x < 5 wrongly drops the endpoints.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 2 · Epp Exercise 2.1.34, p. 52","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §1 Review warm-up","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-027","chapter":"m02","topic":"De Morgan & negation","type":"mcq","q":"Negate: \"If Sara lives in Athens, then she lives in Greece.\"","options":["If Sara does not live in Athens, then she does not live in Greece.","If Sara lives in Athens, then she does not live in Greece.","Sara lives in Athens and she does not live in Greece.","If Sara does not live in Greece, then she does not live in Athens."],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"¬(p → q) ≡ p ∧ ¬q, an and-statement: the negation of an if-then never starts with \"if\". The second option is the inverse and the last is the contrapositive, which is equivalent to the original, not its negation.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 37 · Epp p. 57","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §16 The negation of a conditional statement","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-028","chapter":"m02","topic":"De Morgan & negation","type":"mcq","q":"¬(p → q) ≡","options":["¬p → ¬q","¬p ∧ q","p → ¬q","p ∧ ¬q"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"¬(p → q) ≡ ¬(¬p ∨ q) ≡ ¬¬p ∧ ¬q ≡ p ∧ ¬q (if-then as or, De Morgan, double negative). ¬p → ¬q is the inverse, a conditional and not a negation.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 37 · Epp p. 57","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §16 The negation of a conditional statement","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-029","chapter":"m02","topic":"Conditional, converse, inverse, contrapositive","type":"mcq","q":"Which is the contrapositive of \"If it rained last night, then the sidewalk is wet\"?","options":["If the sidewalk is not wet, then it did not rain last night.","If the sidewalk is wet, then it rained last night.","If it did not rain last night, then the sidewalk is not wet.","It rained last night and the sidewalk is not wet."],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Contrapositive of p → q is ¬q → ¬p. The second option is the converse (q → p), the third the inverse (¬p → ¬q), the fourth the negation.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 41 · Epp p. 57","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §17 The contrapositive","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-030","chapter":"m02","topic":"Conditional, converse, inverse, contrapositive","type":"tf","q":"The converse and the inverse of p → q are logically equivalent to each other.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The inverse ¬p → ¬q is the contrapositive of the converse q → p, so they agree in every row (both F only at p = F, q = T). Neither is equivalent to p → q itself.","source":"2025 Week 2 slides 40–41 · Epp p. 58","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §18 Converse and inverse","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-031","chapter":"m02","topic":"Conditional, converse, inverse, contrapositive","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement form is logically equivalent to p → q?","options":["q → p","¬q → ¬p","¬p → ¬q","q → ¬p"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"A conditional is equivalent to its contrapositive ¬q → ¬p. The converse q → p and the inverse ¬p → ¬q are F at p = F, q = T, where p → q is T; q → ¬p is F at p = q = T.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 38 · Epp p. 57","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §17 The contrapositive","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-032","chapter":"m02","topic":"Conditional, converse, inverse, contrapositive","type":"mcq","q":"Rewrite in if-then form: \"Either you get to work on time or you are fired.\"","options":["If you get to work on time, then you are not fired.","If you are fired, then you did not get to work on time.","If you do not get to work on time, then you are fired.","You get to work on time and you are not fired."],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"With ¬p = on time and q = fired the sentence is ¬p ∨ q ≡ p → q, where p = not on time. The second option is a promise the sentence never makes; the third is the converse.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 36 · Epp p. 56 (Example 2.2.4)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §15 If-then as or","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-033","chapter":"m02","topic":"Conditional, converse, inverse, contrapositive","type":"mcq","q":"p ↔ q ≡","options":["(p → q) ∨ (q → p)","(p → q) ∧ (¬q → ¬p)","(p ∧ q) ∨ ¬p","(p → q) ∧ (q → p)"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"\"p if and only if q\" = (p only if q) ∧ (p if q) = (p → q) ∧ (q → p). The ∨ version is a tautology, and (p → q) ∧ (¬q → ¬p) is just p → q twice.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 42 · Epp p. 60","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §20 The biconditional","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-034","chapter":"m02","topic":"Only if, necessary, sufficient","type":"mcq","q":"b = \"John breaks the world record for the mile\", u = \"John runs the mile in under four minutes\". Symbolise: \"John will break the record only if he runs the mile in under four minutes.\"","options":["b → u","u → b","b ↔ u","¬b → ¬u"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"\"p only if q\" means p → q (equivalently ¬q → ¬p). It does not mean u → b: running under four minutes may still not be fast enough for the record.","source":"Epp p. 59 (Example 2.2.8)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §19 Only if","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-035","chapter":"m02","topic":"Only if, necessary, sufficient","type":"mcq","q":"\"r is a necessary condition for s\" means:","options":["r → s","s → r","r ↔ s","¬s → ¬r"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Necessary: if not r then not s (¬r → ¬s), which by contraposition is s → r. ¬s → ¬r is the contrapositive of r → s, i.e. the sufficient-condition reading in disguise.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 43 · Epp p. 61","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §21 Necessary and sufficient conditions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-036","chapter":"m02","topic":"Only if, necessary, sufficient","type":"tf","q":"\"p only if q\" means the same as \"p if q\".","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"\"p only if q\" is p → q; \"p if q\" is q → p, its converse. Together they give the biconditional p ↔ q.","source":"Epp p. 59","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §19 Only if","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-037","chapter":"m02","topic":"Only if, necessary, sufficient","type":"mcq","q":"Rewrite: \"Being divisible by 3 is a necessary condition for this number to be divisible by 9.\"","options":["If this number is divisible by 3, then it is divisible by 9.","If this number is not divisible by 9, then it is not divisible by 3.","If this number is divisible by 9, then it is divisible by 3.","This number is divisible by 3 if and only if it is divisible by 9."],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"r necessary for s means ¬r → ¬s (not divisible by 3 ⇒ not divisible by 9), whose contrapositive is s → r. The second option makes 3 sufficient for 9, which is false for 6.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 4 · Epp Exercise 2.2.42, p. 65","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §1 Review warm-up","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-038","chapter":"m02","topic":"Only if, necessary, sufficient","type":"mcq","q":"Rewrite in if-then form: \"Ann will go unless it rains.\"","options":["If it rains, then Ann will not go.","If Ann goes, then it does not rain.","If Ann does not go, then it does not rain.","If it does not rain, then Ann will go."],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"\"r unless s\" means \"if not s then r\". The sentence says nothing about what happens when it rains, so \"if it rains, Ann will not go\" adds a claim that is not there.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 5 · Epp Exercise Set 2.2 (definition of unless), p. 65","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §1 Review warm-up","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-039","chapter":"m02","topic":"Only if, necessary, sufficient","type":"mcq","q":"Password rule: \"Your password is valid only if it is at least 8 characters long (q), you have not used it before (r = used before) and it has at least three character types (A).\" With p = valid, which form is right?","options":["p → (q ∧ ¬r ∧ A)","(q ∧ ¬r ∧ A) → p","p ↔ (q ∧ ¬r ∧ A)","p → (q ∧ r ∧ A)"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"\"Valid only if conditions\" makes the conditions necessary: p → conditions. Reversing the arrow would claim any long, new, varied password is automatically valid.","source":"2025 Week 2 slides 10 and 35 · Epp p. 59","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §3 Propositional variables (password rule)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-040","chapter":"m02","topic":"Tautology & contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"¬(p → (p ∨ q)) is:","options":["A tautology","A contradiction (unsatisfiable)","Satisfiable but not a tautology","Logically equivalent to p"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"p → (p ∨ q) is true in every row (generalization), so its negation is false in every row. A satisfiable form needs at least one T row.","source":"2025 Sample Midterm Q2 · Epp p. 48","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm Q2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m02-041","chapter":"m02","topic":"Tautology & contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement form is a tautology?","options":["(p ∨ q) → p","p → (p ∧ q)","(p ∧ q) → p","(p → q) → p"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"If p ∧ q is true then p is true, so the conditional is never T → F. Each other form is F somewhere: (p ∨ q) → p at p = F, q = T; p → (p ∧ q) at p = T, q = F; (p → q) → p at p = F.","source":"Epp p. 48 (definition of tautology)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §11 Tautologies and contradictions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-042","chapter":"m02","topic":"Tautology & contradiction","type":"tf","q":"If t is a tautology, then p ∧ t ≡ t.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"p ∧ t ≡ p (identity law): the conjunction is true exactly when p is. It is p ∨ t that is ≡ t (universal bound).","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 26 · Epp p. 49 (Example 2.1.13)","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §11 Tautologies and contradictions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m02-043","chapter":"m02","topic":"Equivalence laws","type":"yn","q":"Are p → q ∨ r, p ∧ ¬q → r and p ∧ ¬r → q all logically equivalent?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Each one is false only when p is T and q, r are both F; each reduces to ¬p ∨ q ∨ r. This is why \"if p then q or r\" can be proved by assuming p and ¬q.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 6 · Epp Exercise 2.2.14a, p. 63","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §1 Review warm-up","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-001","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity & critical rows","type":"mcq","q":"In the truth-table test for validity, a critical row is a row in which:","options":["the conclusion is true","the conclusion is false","at least one premise is true","all the premises are true"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Critical rows are those with every premise true; only there does the conclusion matter. If the conclusion is false in some critical row, the form is invalid.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 11 · Epp p. 67","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §3 How to check validity","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-002","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity & critical rows","type":"mcq","q":"Argument form:\np → q ∨ ¬r\nq → p ∧ r\n∴ p → r\nWhich row (p, q, r) shows that it is invalid?","options":["T, F, F","T, T, T","F, F, T","T, F, T"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"At p = T, q = F, r = F both premises are true (q ∨ ¬r is T; q → … is vacuously T) and p → r is F. TTT and FFT are critical rows with a true conclusion; TFT is not critical (p → q ∨ ¬r is F there).","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 12 · Epp p. 67 (Example 2.3.1)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §3 Exercise (Example 2.3.1)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-003","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity & critical rows","type":"mcq","q":"p = \"Tom is on team A\", q = \"Hua is on team B\".\n¬p → q\n¬q → p\n∴ ¬p ∨ ¬q\nWhat does the truth table show?","options":["Valid: the conclusion is true in every critical row.","Invalid: at p = T, q = T both premises are true and the conclusion is false.","Invalid: at p = F, q = F both premises are true and the conclusion is false.","Valid: the two premises are contrapositives of each other."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Rows TT, TF, FT are critical; in row TT the conclusion ¬p ∨ ¬q is F, so the form is invalid. At FF both premises are false (not critical). The premises are indeed equivalent to each other, but that says nothing about validity.","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 2 Q3 [official key] · Epp Exercise Set 2.3, p. 77","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §2 Quiz I Section 2, Q3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m03-004","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity & critical rows","type":"mcq","q":"Same argument (¬p → q; ¬q → p; ∴ ¬p ∨ ¬q). How many critical rows does its truth table have?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Both premises are equivalent to p ∨ q, which is true in rows TT, TF, FT: three critical rows. Only FF makes the premises false.","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 2 Q3 [official key]","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §2 Quiz I Section 2, Q3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m03-005","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity & critical rows","type":"tf","q":"An argument form is valid if its conclusion is true in at least one critical row.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Valid requires the conclusion to be true in EVERY critical row. One critical row with a false conclusion is enough to make the form invalid.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 11 · Epp p. 67","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §3 How to check validity","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-006","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity & critical rows","type":"mcq","q":"Which argument form is valid?","options":["p → q;  q;  ∴ p","p → q;  ¬p;  ∴ ¬q","p → q;  q → r;  r;  ∴ p","p ∨ q;  p → r;  q → r;  ∴ r"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"The first is proof by division into cases. The second is the converse error, the third the inverse error, and the fourth is a converse error after transitivity: at p = q = F, r = T all premises hold and p is false.","source":"Epp Table 2.3.1, p. 76","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §11 Summary table","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-007","chapter":"m03","topic":"Modus ponens & tollens","type":"mcq","q":"p → q;  ¬q;  ∴ ¬p  is called:","options":["Modus tollens","Modus ponens","Inverse error","Elimination"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Modus tollens (\"method of denying\"): deny the conclusion, deny the hypothesis. Modus ponens affirms p to get q; the inverse error goes from ¬p to ¬q and is invalid.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 14 · Epp p. 69","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §4 Modus ponens and modus tollens","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-008","chapter":"m03","topic":"Modus ponens & tollens","type":"mcq","q":"Fill in the conclusion so the inference is valid:\nIf √2 is rational, then √2 = a/b for some integers a and b.\nIt is not true that √2 = a/b for some integers a and b.\n∴ ___","options":["√2 is rational.","√2 is not rational.","√2 = a/b for some integers a and b.","No valid conclusion follows."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The second premise denies the conclusion of the conditional, so modus tollens gives the negated hypothesis. Concluding \"√2 is rational\" would contradict the premises.","source":"Epp Exercise Set 2.3 #1, p. 76","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §4 Modus ponens and modus tollens","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-009","chapter":"m03","topic":"Modus ponens & tollens","type":"mcq","q":"Fill in the missing premise so the inference is valid:\nIf they were unsure of the address, then they would have telephoned.\n___\n∴ They were sure of the address.","options":["They telephoned.","They were unsure of the address.","They did not telephone.","If they telephoned, they were unsure of the address."],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"The conclusion is the negated hypothesis, so we need the negated conclusion: modus tollens. \"They telephoned\" would set up the converse error.","source":"Epp Exercise Set 2.3 #5, p. 76","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §4 Modus ponens and modus tollens","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-010","chapter":"m03","topic":"Modus ponens & tollens","type":"mcq","q":"If this graph can be coloured with three colours, then it can be coloured with four colours.\nThis graph cannot be coloured with four colours.\n∴ ___","options":["This graph can be coloured with three colours.","This graph can be coloured with four colours.","This graph cannot be coloured with five colours.","This graph cannot be coloured with three colours."],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Modus tollens: ¬(four colours) with three → four gives ¬(three colours). Nothing at all is said about five colours.","source":"Epp Exercise Set 2.3 #4, p. 76","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §4 Modus ponens and modus tollens","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-011","chapter":"m03","topic":"Modus ponens & tollens","type":"mcq","q":"Why is modus tollens valid even without a truth table?","options":["p → q ≡ ¬q → ¬p, so ¬q with modus ponens on the contrapositive gives ¬p.","p → q ≡ q → p, so ¬q with modus ponens on the converse gives ¬p.","p → q ≡ ¬p → ¬q, so ¬q with modus ponens on the inverse gives ¬p.","p → q ≡ p ∧ q, so ¬q makes the whole conjunction false."],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"A conditional is equivalent to its contrapositive, and modus ponens applied to ¬q → ¬p with ¬q yields ¬p. The converse and the inverse are NOT equivalent to p → q.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 14 · Epp p. 69","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §4 Modus ponens and modus tollens","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-012","chapter":"m03","topic":"Rules of inference","type":"mcq","q":"x − 3 = 0 or x + 2 = 0.\nx is not negative, so x + 2 ≠ 0.\n∴ x − 3 = 0.\nWhich rule of inference is this?","options":["Modus tollens","Elimination","Proof by division into cases","Specialization"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Elimination: p ∨ q, ¬q ∴ p. Two possibilities, one ruled out, the other holds. Division into cases needs both alternatives to lead to the same conclusion.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 15 · Epp p. 71 (Example 2.3.6)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §5 Additional rules of inference","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-013","chapter":"m03","topic":"Rules of inference","type":"mcq","q":"Anton is a junior.\n∴ Anton is a junior or Anton is a senior.\nWhich rule is this?","options":["Specialization","Conjunction","Generalization","Elimination"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Generalization: p ∴ p ∨ q (adding an \"or\" alternative keeps it true). Specialization goes the other way, from p ∧ q down to p.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 15 · Epp p. 70 (Example 2.3.4)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §5 Additional rules of inference","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-014","chapter":"m03","topic":"Rules of inference","type":"mcq","q":"Ana knows numerical analysis and Ana knows graph algorithms.\n∴ Ana knows graph algorithms.\nWhich rule is this?","options":["Generalization","Elimination","Conjunction","Specialization"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Specialization: p ∧ q ∴ q. Conjunction is the reverse, building p ∧ q from p and q separately.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 15 · Epp p. 70 (Example 2.3.5)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §5 Additional rules of inference","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-015","chapter":"m03","topic":"Rules of inference","type":"mcq","q":"If n is divisible by 18, then n is divisible by 9.\nIf n is divisible by 9, then the sum of the digits of n is divisible by 9.\n∴ If n is divisible by 18, then the sum of the digits of n is divisible by 9.\nWhich rule is this?","options":["Transitivity","Modus ponens","Proof by division into cases","Generalization"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Transitivity: p → q, q → r ∴ p → r. Modus ponens needs p itself as a premise and concludes q, not a conditional.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 16 · Epp p. 71 (Example 2.3.7)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §5 Additional rules of inference","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-016","chapter":"m03","topic":"Rules of inference","type":"mcq","q":"x is positive or x is negative.\nIf x is positive, then x² > 0.\nIf x is negative, then x² > 0.\n∴ x² > 0.\nWhich rule is this?","options":["Elimination","Proof by division into cases","Transitivity","Modus ponens"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"p ∨ q, p → r, q → r ∴ r: whichever case holds, the same conclusion follows. Elimination would need one case ruled out.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 16 · Epp p. 71","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §5 Additional rules of inference","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-017","chapter":"m03","topic":"Rules of inference","type":"mcq","q":"Which argument form is the contradiction rule (c = a contradiction)?","options":["c → p;  ∴ p","p → c;  ∴ p","¬p → c;  ∴ p","p ∧ ¬p;  ∴ c"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"If supposing p false leads to a contradiction, p is true: ¬p → c ∴ p, the logic behind proof by contradiction. c → p is vacuously true for any p, so it proves nothing; p → c actually gives ¬p.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 24 · Epp p. 75","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §9 Contradictions and valid arguments","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-018","chapter":"m03","topic":"Rules of inference","type":"mcq","q":"Which form is the rule called \"conjunction\" in Epp's Table 2.3.1?","options":["p ∧ q;  ∴ p","p;  ∴ p ∨ q","p ∨ q;  ¬q;  ∴ p","p;  q;  ∴ p ∧ q"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Conjunction combines two established lines into one. The others are specialization, generalization and elimination. It is only in the summary table, not on slides 15–16, yet the 2025 quiz deduction needed it.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 26 · Epp Table 2.3.1, p. 76","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §11 Summary table","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-019","chapter":"m03","topic":"Fallacies","type":"mcq","q":"If Zeke is a cheater, then Zeke sits in the back row.\nZeke sits in the back row.\n∴ Zeke is a cheater.\nThis argument commits:","options":["The converse error","The inverse error","No error: it is modus ponens","No error: it is modus tollens"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"p → q, q ∴ p affirms the consequent; it would only follow from the converse q → p. A non-cheater in the back row makes the premises true and the conclusion false.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 20 · Epp p. 73 (Example 2.3.9)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §7 Fallacies","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-020","chapter":"m03","topic":"Fallacies","type":"mcq","q":"If these two vertices are adjacent, then they do not have the same colour.\nThese two vertices are not adjacent.\n∴ These two vertices have the same colour.\nThis argument commits:","options":["The converse error","The inverse error","No error: it is modus tollens","No error: it is elimination"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"p → q, ¬p ∴ ¬q denies the antecedent; it would need the inverse ¬p → ¬q. It looks like modus tollens, but modus tollens denies the conclusion q, not the hypothesis.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 21 · Epp p. 73 (Example 2.3.10)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §7 Fallacies","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-021","chapter":"m03","topic":"Fallacies","type":"mcq","q":"The converse error is also called the fallacy of:","options":["Denying the antecedent","Circular reasoning","Affirming the consequent","Jumping to a conclusion"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Converse error: p → q, q ∴ p, so the consequent q is affirmed. Denying the antecedent is the inverse error (p → q, ¬p ∴ ¬q).","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 20 · Epp p. 72","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §7 Fallacies","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-022","chapter":"m03","topic":"Fallacies","type":"mcq","q":"Which row (p, q) shows that p → q;  q;  ∴ p  is invalid?","options":["T, F","F, F","T, T","F, T"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"At p = F, q = T both premises are true but p is false. At TF the premise p → q is false and at FF the premise q is false, so those rows are not critical; TT is critical with a true conclusion.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 21 · Epp p. 73","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §7 Fallacies (truth-table verification)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-023","chapter":"m03","topic":"Fallacies","type":"tf","q":"p → q;  ¬p;  ∴ ¬q  is a valid argument form.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"It is the inverse error. At p = F, q = T both premises are true and ¬q is false.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 21 · Epp p. 73","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §7 Fallacies","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-024","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity vs truth & soundness","type":"mcq","q":"An argument is called sound if and only if:","options":["it is valid and all its premises are true","all its premises and its conclusion are true","it is valid and its conclusion is true","its form appears in Epp's Table 2.3.1"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Sound = valid + true premises; only then is the conclusion guaranteed true. A valid argument with a false premise is unsound even if its conclusion happens to be true, and an invalid argument is unsound even if every statement in it is true.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 23 · Epp p. 74","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §8 Validity vs truth; soundness","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-025","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity vs truth & soundness","type":"mcq","q":"If Canada is north of the United States, then temperatures in Canada never rise above freezing.\nCanada is north of the United States.\n∴ Temperatures in Canada never rise above freezing.\nWhich description is correct?","options":["Invalid, because the conclusion is false.","Valid but unsound: the first premise is false.","Sound, because it has the form of modus ponens.","Invalid: it commits the converse error."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The form is modus ponens, so it is valid; but the first premise is false, so it is unsound and the conclusion need not be true. A false conclusion never by itself makes a form invalid.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 22 · Epp p. 74 (Example 2.3.11)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §8 Validity vs truth; soundness","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-026","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity vs truth & soundness","type":"tf","q":"\"If New York is a big city, then New York has tall buildings. New York has tall buildings. ∴ New York is a big city.\" This argument is invalid even though its premises and conclusion are all true.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Its form is the converse error (p → q, q ∴ p), which is invalid. Validity depends only on form; true statements do not rescue a bad form.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 23 · Epp p. 74 (Example 2.3.12)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §8 Validity vs truth; soundness","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-027","chapter":"m03","topic":"Validity vs truth & soundness","type":"tf","q":"It is impossible for a valid argument to have all premises true and a false conclusion.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"That is exactly what validity rules out. A valid form can still have a false conclusion, but only when at least one premise is false (the Canada example).","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 10 · Epp p. 66","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §2 Arguments, argument forms, validity","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-028","chapter":"m03","topic":"Deduction chains","type":"mcq","q":"Premises:\na. ¬p ∨ q → r\nb. s ∨ ¬q\nc. ¬t\nd. p → t\ne. ¬p ∧ r → ¬s\n∴ ¬q\nThe first step derives ¬p. From which premises and by which rule?","options":["d and c, modus ponens","a and b, elimination","d and c, modus tollens","e and c, modus tollens"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"p → t (d) with ¬t (c) gives ¬p by modus tollens. ⚠️ The 2025 slide and Epp's Appendix B write \"¬p by premise (c)\"; premise (c) is ¬t.","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 1 Q3 [official key] · Epp Exercise 2.3.41, p. 79","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §1 Quiz I Section 1, Q3 · archive-2025/AE-errata-2025.md — A03","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m03-029","chapter":"m03","topic":"Deduction chains","type":"mcq","q":"Same premises (a–e, ∴ ¬q). From ¬p the solution writes ¬p ∨ q, so that premise a can be used. Which rule gives ¬p ∨ q?","options":["Specialization","Elimination","Conjunction","Generalization"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"p ∴ p ∨ q is generalization. It is needed because premise a's hypothesis is the disjunction ¬p ∨ q; modus ponens then gives r.","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 1 Q3 [official key]","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §1 Quiz I Section 1, Q3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m03-030","chapter":"m03","topic":"Deduction chains","type":"mcq","q":"Same premises (a–e, ∴ ¬q). After getting ¬s, the last step uses premise b (s ∨ ¬q) to reach ¬q. Which rule?","options":["Elimination","Modus tollens","Proof by division into cases","Specialization"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"s ∨ ¬q with ¬s rules out the first alternative, leaving ¬q: elimination. There is no conditional here, so modus tollens does not apply.","source":"2025 Quiz I Sec. 1 Q3 [official key]","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §1 Quiz I Section 1, Q3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m03-031","chapter":"m03","topic":"Deduction chains","type":"mcq","q":"Premises:\na. p → q\nb. r ∨ s\nc. ¬s → ¬t\nd. ¬q ∨ s\ne. ¬s\nf. ¬p ∧ r → u\ng. w ∨ t\n∴ u ∧ w\nHow is ¬t obtained?","options":["From c and e, by modus tollens","From c and e, by modus ponens","From g and e, by elimination","From b and e, by elimination"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"¬s → ¬t with ¬s affirms the hypothesis, so modus ponens gives ¬t (the negation signs do not make it modus tollens). Then w ∨ t with ¬t gives w by elimination.","source":"2025 Sample Quiz Q3 · Epp Exercise Set 2.3, p. 79","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §3 Sample Quiz Q3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m03-032","chapter":"m03","topic":"Deduction chains","type":"mcq","q":"Pirate treasure. l: the house is next to a lake; k: treasure in the kitchen; e: front-yard tree is an elm; f: treasure under the flagpole; o: back-yard tree is an oak; g: treasure in the garage.\na. l → ¬k\nb. e → k\nc. l\nd. e ∨ f\ne. o → g\nWhere is the treasure?","options":["In the kitchen","In the garage","Under the flagpole","It cannot be determined"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"l → ¬k, l ∴ ¬k (modus ponens); e → k, ¬k ∴ ¬e (modus tollens); e ∨ f, ¬e ∴ f (elimination). The garage premise is never usable because nothing says the back-yard tree is an oak.","source":"2025 Sample Midterm Q4 · Epp Exercise 2.3.37, p. 78","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm Q4","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m03-033","chapter":"m03","topic":"Deduction chains","type":"mcq","q":"Where are the glasses?\na. RK → GK (reading in kitchen → glasses on kitchen table)\nb. GK → SB (→ saw them at breakfast)\nc. ¬SB\nd. RL ∨ RK (reading in living room or kitchen)\ne. RL → GC (→ glasses on coffee table)\nWhich sequence of rules reaches GC?","options":["Modus ponens, modus tollens, elimination, transitivity","Transitivity, modus ponens, division into cases, modus ponens","Elimination, transitivity, modus tollens, modus ponens","Transitivity, modus tollens, elimination, modus ponens"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"RK → SB (transitivity of a, b); with ¬SB, ¬RK (modus tollens); with RL ∨ RK, RL (elimination); with RL → GC, GC (modus ponens). This layout is what the quiz deduction question asks you to reproduce.","source":"2025 Week 3 slides 17–19 · Epp p. 71 (Example 2.3.8)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §6 Application: where are the glasses?","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m03-034","chapter":"m03","topic":"Deduction chains","type":"mcq","q":"Knights always tell the truth, knaves always lie. A says: \"B is a knight.\" B says: \"A and I are of opposite type.\" What are A and B?","options":["Both are knaves","Both are knights","A is a knight, B is a knave","A is a knave, B is a knight"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"If A were a knight, B would be a knight, and then B's claim of opposite types would be true: a contradiction. So A is a knave, A's claim is false, and B is a knave; B's claim is then false, as a knave's must be.","source":"2025 Week 3 slide 25 · Epp p. 75 (Example 2.3.14)","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §10 Knights and knaves","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-001","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"tf","q":"D = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}.\n∀x ∈ D, x² ≥ x.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Method of exhaustion: 1 ≥ 1, 4 ≥ 2, 9 ≥ 3, 16 ≥ 4, 25 ≥ 5 — every element works, so the universal statement is true. A finite domain can be checked element by element.","source":"Epp Example 3.1.3(a), p. 110 · 2025 wk3 slide 8","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §5 Truth and falsity of universal statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-002","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"mcq","q":"Which value of x is a counterexample to\n∀x ∈ ℝ, x² ≥ x ?","options":["x = −½","x = 1","x = ½","x = 2"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"(½)² = ¼ < ½, so x² ≥ x fails at ½. For x = −½ the square ¼ is still ≥ −½ — negative x never break this inequality.","source":"Epp Example 3.1.3(b), p. 110 · 2025 wk3 slide 8","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §5 Truth and falsity of universal statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-003","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"tf","q":"E = {5, 6, 7, 8}.\n∃m ∈ E such that m² = m.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"25, 36, 49, 64 differ from 5, 6, 7, 8, so no element works and the existential statement is false. To refute ∃ you must check every element; the witness m = 1 is not in E.","source":"Epp Example 3.1.4(b), p. 112 · 2025 wk3 slide 10","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §7 Truth and falsity of existential statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-004","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"mcq","q":"Q(n): \"n is a factor of 8\", with domain ℤ.\nWhat is the truth set of Q(n)?","options":["{±1, ±2, ±4, ±8}","{1, 2, 4, 8}","{2, 4, 8}","{0, 1, 2, 4, 8}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Over ℤ the negative divisors also divide 8 (8 = (−2)(−4)), so the truth set has eight elements. {1, 2, 4, 8} is the answer for domain ℤ⁺ — the truth set depends on the domain.","source":"Epp Example 3.1.2, p. 109 · 2025 wk3 slide 5","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §3 Truth sets","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-005","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"mcq","q":"P(x): x² > x, domain ℝ.\nWhich of P(2), P(½), P(−½) are true?","options":["P(2) only","P(2) and P(½) only","All three","P(2) and P(−½) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"4 > 2 true; ¼ > ½ false; ¼ > −½ true. The trap is assuming every negative input fails: a square is never negative, so it beats any negative number.","source":"Epp Example 3.1.1, p. 109 · 2025 wk3 slide 4","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §3 Truth sets","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-006","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"mcq","q":"Which task can be completed by exhibiting just ONE element of the domain D?","options":["Showing that ∀x ∈ D, Q(x) is true","Showing that ∀x ∈ D, Q(x) is false","Showing that ∃x ∈ D such that Q(x) is false","Showing that ∀x ∈ D, if P(x) then Q(x) is vacuously true"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"One counterexample refutes a universal statement. Proving ∀ true, refuting ∃, or establishing vacuous truth all require every element of D (exhaustion or a general argument).","source":"Epp pp. 110–112 (definitions of truth of ∀ and ∃)","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §5 Truth and falsity of universal statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-007","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"mcq","q":"D = {−2, −1, 0, 1, 2}. Which statement is true?","options":["∀x ∈ D, x³ ≥ x","∀x ∈ D, x² ≥ x","∃x ∈ D such that x² < 0","∀x ∈ D, x² > x"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"x² ≥ x holds at all five points (4 ≥ −2, 1 ≥ −1, 0 ≥ 0, 1 ≥ 1, 4 ≥ 2). x³ ≥ x fails at −2 (−8 < −2), and x² > x fails at 0 and 1.","source":"Verified by exhaustion over D (pattern of Epp Example 3.1.3)","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §5 Truth and falsity of universal statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-008","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"tf","q":"D = {1, 3, 5, 7}.\n∀x ∈ D, if x is even then x > 100.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"No element of D is even, so the hypothesis is false for every x and the statement is vacuously true (true by default). Its negation \"∃x ∈ D that is even and ≤ 100\" is false.","source":"Epp §3.2 Vacuous Truth, p. 126","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §12 Not on the slides but in the assigned section","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-009","chapter":"m04","topic":"Truth of ∀ and ∃","type":"mcq","q":"In the instruction \"Solve 3x − 4 = 5\", how is x quantified?","options":["Universally: for every real x, 3x − 4 = 5","It is not quantified, so it is not a statement","Universally, but only over the integers","Existentially: show ∃ a real x with 3x − 4 = 5"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"\"Solve\" asks you to find a value, i.e. show some real x works (x = 3). Contrast (x + 1)² = x² + 2x + 1, which is implicitly universal.","source":"2025 wk3 slide 11 · Epp pp. 116–117","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §8 Implicit quantification","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-010","chapter":"m04","topic":"Negating quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"¬(∀x ∈ D, Q(x)) is logically equivalent to …","options":["∃x ∈ D such that ¬Q(x)","∀x ∈ D, ¬Q(x)","∃x ∈ D such that Q(x)","¬(∃x ∈ D such that ¬Q(x))"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Theorem 3.2.1: \"not all are\" means \"some is not\". ∀x, ¬Q(x) is far stronger than the negation, and ¬(∃x ¬Q(x)) is equivalent to the original statement, not its negation.","source":"Epp Theorem 3.2.1, p. 122 · 2025 wk3 slide 12","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §9 Negations of quantified statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-011","chapter":"m04","topic":"Negating quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"Negate: \"All mathematicians wear glasses.\"","options":["No mathematicians wear glasses.","Some mathematicians wear glasses.","There is at least one mathematician who does not wear glasses.","All people who wear glasses are mathematicians."],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"A single mathematician without glasses already makes \"all\" false, so the negation claims exactly that. \"No mathematicians wear glasses\" is the classic wrong answer — it is much stronger.","source":"Epp §3.2, p. 122 · 2025 wk3 slide 12","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §9 Negations of quantified statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-012","chapter":"m04","topic":"Negating quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"Negate:\n∀ real numbers x, if x² ≥ 1 then x > 0.","options":["∃x ∈ ℝ such that x² ≥ 1 and x < 0","∀x ∈ ℝ, if x² ≥ 1 then x ≤ 0","∃x ∈ ℝ such that if x² ≥ 1 then x ≤ 0","∃x ∈ ℝ such that x² ≥ 1 and x ≤ 0"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"¬(∀x, P(x) → Q(x)) ≡ ∃x, P(x) ∧ ¬Q(x), and ¬(x > 0) is x ≤ 0 (not x < 0). The negation of an if-then is an and, never another if-then.","source":"2025 Quiz I Section 2, Q1 (official key)","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §2 Quiz I — Section 2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m04-013","chapter":"m04","topic":"Negating quantifiers","type":"tf","q":"The negation of\n\"∀ integers n, if n² is even then n is even\"\nis\n\"∀ integers n, if n² is even then n is not even.\"","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The negation is existential and uses and: ∃ an integer n such that n² is even and n is not even. Keeping ∀ and the if-then is Epp's flagged wrong answer.","source":"Epp §3.2 exercises 11–14 caution, p. 129","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §10 Negation of universal conditional statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-014","chapter":"m04","topic":"Negating quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"Negate: \"If a computer program has more than 100,000 lines, then it contains a bug.\"","options":["If a program has at most 100,000 lines, then it contains no bug.","There is a program with more than 100,000 lines that contains no bug.","Every program with more than 100,000 lines contains no bug.","There is a program with at most 100,000 lines that contains a bug."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The statement is implicitly ∀; its negation is ∃ a program with the hypothesis true (> 100,000 lines) and the conclusion false (no bug). Option 2 is the inverse, not the negation.","source":"Epp Example 3.2.4(b), p. 125 · 2025 wk3 slide 14","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §10 Negation of universal conditional statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-015","chapter":"m04","topic":"Nested quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"\"Everybody loves somebody\" is ∀ people x, ∃ a person y such that x loves y.\nWhich is its negation?","options":["∀ people x, ∃ a person y such that x does not love y","∃ a person y such that ∀ people x, x does not love y","∃ a person x such that ∀ people y, x does not love y","∀ people x, ∀ people y, x does not love y"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Negate left to right: ∀ becomes ∃, ∃ becomes ∀, and the predicate is negated — \"somebody loves nobody\". Option 2 (\"everyone fails to love someone\") only flips the predicate.","source":"2025 Quiz I Section 1, Q1 (official key) · sample midterm Q5","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §1 Quiz I — Section 1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m04-016","chapter":"m04","topic":"Nested quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"¬(∃x ∈ D such that ∀y ∈ E, P(x, y)) is logically equivalent to …","options":["∀x ∈ D, ∃y ∈ E such that ¬P(x, y)","∃x ∈ D such that ∀y ∈ E, ¬P(x, y)","∀x ∈ D, ∀y ∈ E, ¬P(x, y)","∃x ∈ D such that ∃y ∈ E such that ¬P(x, y)"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Each quantifier flips (∃ → ∀, ∀ → ∃) and P is negated. ∀x ∀y ¬P is too strong: it says P fails for every pair.","source":"Epp §3.3, p. 137 (Negations of Statements with Two Different Quantifiers)","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §1 Warm-up: more on multiple quantifiers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-017","chapter":"m04","topic":"Nested quantifiers","type":"tf","q":"∀x ∈ ℤ, ∃y ∈ ℤ such that x + y = 0.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Given any x, choose y = −x (an integer); y is allowed to depend on x because ∃y comes after ∀x.","source":"Epp §3.3 interpretation rule, p. 133","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §1 Warm-up: more on multiple quantifiers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-018","chapter":"m04","topic":"Nested quantifiers","type":"tf","q":"∃y ∈ ℤ such that ∀x ∈ ℤ, x + y = 0.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Here one y must work for every x; y = −1 works for x = 1 but not for x = 2. Swapping ∀ and ∃ turned a true statement into a false one.","source":"Epp §3.3 Order of Quantifiers, p. 138","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §1 Warm-up: more on multiple quantifiers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-019","chapter":"m04","topic":"Nested quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"D = {1, 2, 3}. Which statement is true?","options":["∀x ∈ D, ∃y ∈ D such that y < x","∃x ∈ D such that ∀y ∈ D, x < y","∃x ∈ D such that ∀y ∈ D, x ≤ y","∀x ∈ D, ∀y ∈ D, x ≤ y"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"x = 1 satisfies 1 ≤ y for every y. The strict version fails because y can equal x (1 < 1 is false), and ∀x ∃y (y < x) fails at x = 1.","source":"Verified by exhaustion over D (pattern of Epp §3.3, pp. 132–133)","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §1 Warm-up: more on multiple quantifiers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-020","chapter":"m04","topic":"Nested quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"D = {1, 2, 3, 4}.\n(I) ∀x ∈ D, ∃y ∈ D such that x + y is even.\n(II) ∃y ∈ D such that ∀x ∈ D, x + y is even.\nWhich are true?","options":["(I) only","(II) only","Both (I) and (II)","Neither"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(I): for each x pick y = x, so x + y = 2x is even. (II): any fixed y has the wrong parity for half of D, e.g. y = 1 with x = 2 gives 3.","source":"Verified by exhaustion over D (pattern of Epp Example 3.3.9, p. 139)","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §1 Warm-up: more on multiple quantifiers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-021","chapter":"m04","topic":"Nested quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"To establish that \"∀x ∈ D, ∃y ∈ E such that P(x, y)\" is true, you must …","options":["find one y that works for every x in D","find one x and one y such that P(x, y) is true","check that P(x, y) holds for every pair (x, y)","let anyone pick x, then find a y (possibly depending on x) that works"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Quantifiers act in the order written: x is handed to you first, then you choose y. A single y for all x is the stronger ∃y ∀x statement.","source":"Epp §3.3 box Interpreting Statements with Two Different Quantifiers, p. 133","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §1 Warm-up: more on multiple quantifiers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-022","chapter":"m04","topic":"Nested quantifiers","type":"tf","q":"∃x ∈ ℝ such that ∀y ∈ ℝ, x·y = 0.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Choose x = 0: then 0·y = 0 for every real y. One x working against every y is exactly what ∃x ∀y asks for.","source":"Epp §3.3 interpretation rule, p. 133","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §1 Warm-up: more on multiple quantifiers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-023","chapter":"m04","topic":"Conditional variants","type":"mcq","q":"What is the contrapositive of\n∀x ∈ ℝ, if x > 2 then x² > 4 ?","options":["∀x ∈ ℝ, if x² > 4 then x > 2","∀x ∈ ℝ, if x² ≤ 4 then x ≤ 2","∀x ∈ ℝ, if x ≤ 2 then x² ≤ 4","∃x ∈ ℝ such that x > 2 and x² ≤ 4"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Contrapositive: negate and swap — if ¬Q(x) then ¬P(x). Option 2 is the converse, option 3 the inverse, option 4 the negation.","source":"Epp Example 3.2.5, p. 127","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §12 Not on the slides but in the assigned section","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-024","chapter":"m04","topic":"Conditional variants","type":"mcq","q":"The converse of \"∀x ∈ ℝ, if x > 2 then x² > 4\" is false.\nWhich x is a counterexample to the converse?","options":["x = 3","x = −3","x = 2","x = 0"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Converse: if x² > 4 then x > 2. At x = −3, x² = 9 > 4 but −3 ≤ 2. At x = 2 and x = 0 the hypothesis x² > 4 is false, so they cannot refute it.","source":"Epp Example 3.2.5, p. 127","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §12 Not on the slides but in the assigned section","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-025","chapter":"m04","topic":"Conditional variants","type":"mcq","q":"Start from \"∀ integers n, if 6 | n then 3 | n\". Which of these is FALSE?","options":["The statement itself","Its contrapositive: ∀ n, if 3 ∤ n then 6 ∤ n","∀ n, if 6 | n then n is even","Its converse: ∀ n, if 3 | n then 6 | n"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"The converse fails at n = 3 (3 | 3 but 6 ∤ 3). The original and its contrapositive are equivalent, so they stand or fall together — both true here.","source":"Epp §3.2 converse/contrapositive, p. 127; checked for |n| ≤ 300","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §12 Not on the slides but in the assigned section","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-026","chapter":"m04","topic":"Necessary, sufficient, only if","type":"mcq","q":"\"∀x, r(x) is a necessary condition for s(x)\" means …","options":["∀x, if s(x) then r(x)","∀x, if r(x) then s(x)","∀x, r(x) if and only if s(x)","∃x such that r(x) and s(x)"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Necessary: without r(x) you cannot have s(x), i.e. if ¬r(x) then ¬s(x), whose contrapositive is if s(x) then r(x). \"If r then s\" is what sufficient means.","source":"Epp §3.2 Necessary and Sufficient Conditions, p. 128","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §11 Negating necessary and sufficient conditions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-027","chapter":"m04","topic":"Necessary, sufficient, only if","type":"mcq","q":"\"∀x, r(x) only if s(x)\" means …","options":["∀x, if s(x) then r(x)","∀x, if ¬r(x) then ¬s(x)","∀x, if r(x) then s(x)","∀x, r(x) and s(x)"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"\"r only if s\": r can occur only when s does, so if ¬s(x) then ¬r(x), equivalently if r(x) then s(x). Reading it as \"if s then r\" is the usual reversal.","source":"Epp §3.2, p. 128 · Example 3.2.7","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §11 Negating necessary and sufficient conditions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-028","chapter":"m04","topic":"Necessary, sufficient, only if","type":"mcq","q":"Negate: \"Squareness is a sufficient condition for rectangularity.\"","options":["There is a figure that is a rectangle and is not a square.","For every figure x, if x is a square then x is not a rectangle.","For every figure x, if x is not a square then x is not a rectangle.","There is a figure that is a square and is not a rectangle."],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Rewrite first: ∀x, if x is a square then x is a rectangle. The negation is ∃x, square and not rectangle. Option 2 negates the converse (the necessary reading).","source":"Epp Example 3.2.6(a), p. 128 · 2025 wk3 slide 15","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §11 Negating necessary and sufficient conditions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-029","chapter":"m04","topic":"Necessary, sufficient, only if","type":"mcq","q":"Negate: \"Being at least 35 years old is a necessary condition for being president of the United States.\"","options":["There is a person who is at least 35 and is not president.","There is a person who is president and is younger than 35.","There is a person who is younger than 35 and is not president.","Every person who is at least 35 is president."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Necessary means ∀x, if x is president then x is at least 35; the negation is ∃x, president and younger than 35. Option 2 treats \"at least 35\" as sufficient.","source":"Epp Example 3.2.6(b), p. 128 · 2025 wk3 slide 15","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §11 Negating necessary and sufficient conditions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-030","chapter":"m04","topic":"Necessary, sufficient, only if","type":"mcq","q":"For integers n, which statement is true?","options":["\"4 | n\" is a necessary condition for \"n is even\"","\"n is odd\" is a sufficient condition for \"4 | n\"","\"4 | n\" is a sufficient condition for \"n is even\"","\"n is even\" is necessary and sufficient for \"4 | n\""],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Sufficient: if 4 | n then n is even — true (n = 4k = 2(2k)). Necessary would mean every even n is divisible by 4, which fails at n = 2.","source":"Epp §3.2, p. 128; checked for |n| ≤ 400","study":"archive-2025/A04-quantified-statements.md — §11 Negating necessary and sufficient conditions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-031","chapter":"m04","topic":"Arguments with quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"If an integer is even, then its square is even.\nk is a particular integer that is even.\n∴ k² is even.\nWhich form does this argument have?","options":["Universal modus ponens (valid)","Universal modus tollens (valid)","Converse error (invalid)","Inverse error (invalid)"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Major premise ∀x, E(x) → S(x); minor premise E(k); conclusion S(k): affirm the hypothesis for a particular k. Modus tollens would instead deny the conclusion.","source":"Epp Example 3.4.1, pp. 147–148","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §4 Modus ponens and modus tollens","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-032","chapter":"m04","topic":"Arguments with quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"All human beings are mortal.\nFelix is mortal.\n∴ Felix is a human being.\nThis argument is …","options":["valid — universal modus ponens","invalid — inverse error","invalid — converse error","valid — universal modus tollens"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"It affirms the conclusion Q(a) and infers P(a); Felix could be a cat inside the mortals disk but outside the humans disk. The inverse error instead starts from ¬P(a).","source":"Epp Example 3.4.6, pp. 152–153","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §7 Fallacies","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-033","chapter":"m04","topic":"Arguments with quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"All professors are absent-minded.\nTom Hutchins is not absent-minded.\nWhat follows?","options":["Tom Hutchins is not a professor.","Tom Hutchins is a professor.","Nothing can be concluded about Tom Hutchins.","Some professors are not absent-minded."],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Universal modus tollens: ∀x, P(x) → A(x) and ¬A(Tom) give ¬P(Tom). This is a valid form, so a conclusion does follow.","source":"Epp Example 3.4.4, p. 150","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §4 Modus ponens and modus tollens","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-034","chapter":"m04","topic":"Arguments with quantifiers","type":"tf","q":"The argument form\n∀x, if P(x) then Q(x).\n¬P(a) for a particular a.\n∴ ¬Q(a).\nis valid.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"This is the inverse error: a can fail P yet still satisfy Q (\"all humans are mortal; my cat is not human; ∴ my cat is not mortal\"). A conditional is not equivalent to its inverse.","source":"Epp §3.4 Inverse Error (Quantified Form), p. 153","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §7 Fallacies","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m04-035","chapter":"m04","topic":"Arguments with quantifiers","type":"mcq","q":"No polynomial functions have horizontal asymptotes.\nThis function has a horizontal asymptote.\n∴ This function is not a polynomial function.\nThis argument is …","options":["invalid — converse error","invalid — inverse error","valid — universal modus ponens","valid — universal modus tollens"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Rewrite \"no\" as ∀x, if x is polynomial then x has no horizontal asymptote; the minor premise denies the conclusion, so ¬P(a) follows by universal modus tollens.","source":"Epp Example 3.4.7, pp. 153–154","study":"archive-2025/A03-valid-invalid-arguments.md — §4 Modus ponens and modus tollens","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-001","chapter":"m05","topic":"Even, odd, prime","type":"mcq","q":"Under Epp's definitions, the integer 1 is …","options":["prime","composite","neither prime nor composite","both prime and composite"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Both definitions start with n > 1, so 1 is excluded from each. The 2025 slide cropped off the n > 1 header — without it the prime condition would wrongly admit 1.","source":"Epp Example 4.1.2(a), p. 163 · 2025 wk5 slide 14","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §3 Even, odd, prime, composite","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-002","chapter":"m05","topic":"Even, odd, prime","type":"mcq","q":"If a and b are integers, is 10a + 8b + 1 odd?","options":["Yes: it equals 2(5a + 4b) + 1 and 5a + 4b ∈ ℤ","No: 10a + 8b is even, so the total is even","Only when a and b are both odd","It cannot be decided without values for a and b"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Factor out 2 and note 5a + 4b is an integer (closure of ℤ), then apply the definition of odd. No values of a, b are needed — that is the point of the definition.","source":"Epp Example 4.1.1(d), p. 162 · 2025 wk5 slide 13","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §3 Even, odd, prime, composite","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-003","chapter":"m05","topic":"Even, odd, prime","type":"tf","q":"0 is neither even nor odd.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"0 is even: 0 = 2·0 and 0 is an integer, so it satisfies the definition of even exactly. Zero being \"not positive\" has nothing to do with parity.","source":"Epp Example 4.1.1(a), p. 162","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §3 Even, odd, prime, composite","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-004","chapter":"m05","topic":"Even, odd, prime","type":"mcq","q":"−301 = 2k + 1 shows −301 is odd. Which integer k works?","options":["k = −150","k = 150","None — −301 is even","k = −151"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"2(−151) + 1 = −302 + 1 = −301. With k = −150 you get −299; negative odd numbers still fit the form 2k + 1 with k negative.","source":"Epp Example 4.1.1(b), p. 162","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §3 Even, odd, prime, composite","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-005","chapter":"m05","topic":"Even, odd, prime","type":"mcq","q":"Which list contains only composite numbers?","options":["1, 4, 6, 8","4, 6, 9, 15","2, 4, 6, 8","9, 15, 21, 23"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"4 = 2·2, 6 = 2·3, 9 = 3·3, 15 = 3·5. 1 is not composite (n > 1 required), 2 is prime, and 23 is prime.","source":"Epp §4.1 definition of composite, p. 163","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §3 Even, odd, prime, composite","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-006","chapter":"m05","topic":"Even, odd, prime","type":"tf","q":"The product of any two odd integers is odd.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(2k + 1)(2l + 1) = 4kl + 2k + 2l + 1 = 2(2kl + k + l) + 1 with 2kl + k + l ∈ ℤ, so the product is odd.","source":"2025 sample midterm Q6a","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m05-007","chapter":"m05","topic":"Rational numbers","type":"mcq","q":"Let x = 0.121212… (12 repeating). Which fraction equals x?","options":["12/100","12/99","121/999","1/12"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"100x = 12.1212…, so 100x − x = 12 and 99x = 12, x = 12/99. 12/100 = 0.12 exactly, which is a terminating decimal.","source":"Epp Example 4.3.1, p. 183 · 2025 wk5 slide 44","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §13 Rational numbers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-008","chapter":"m05","topic":"Rational numbers","type":"mcq","q":"Is 2/0 rational or irrational?","options":["Rational — 2 and 0 are both integers","Irrational — it cannot be written with a nonzero denominator","Rational, because 2/0 = 0","Neither — 2/0 is not a real number"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Division by 0 is undefined, so 2/0 is not a real number at all; irrational numbers are real numbers that are not rational, so \"irrational\" does not apply either.","source":"Epp Example 4.3.1, p. 183 · 2025 wk5 slide 44","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §13 Rational numbers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-009","chapter":"m05","topic":"Rational numbers","type":"mcq","q":"Proving r + s is rational with r = a/b, s = c/d: you write r + s = (ad + bc)/(bd). Why is bd ≠ 0?","options":["Zero product property: b ≠ 0 and d ≠ 0","ℤ is closed under multiplication","Definition of rational applied to r + s","b and d are positive"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The zero product property says a product of two nonzero reals is nonzero. Closure only shows bd is an integer — it says nothing about whether bd is 0. This is the step students most often omit.","source":"Epp Theorem 4.3.2 proof, p. 186 · 2025 wk5 slide 46","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §13 Rational numbers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-010","chapter":"m05","topic":"Rational numbers","type":"mcq","q":"True or false: \"The ratio of any two rational numbers is a rational number.\"","options":["True: (a/b)/(c/d) = ad/(bc)","False: 1 and 2 are rational, but 1/2 is not an integer","False: 1 and 0 are rational, but 1/0 is undefined","False: √2 and 1 give √2, which is irrational"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"The divisor may be 0, and 1/0 is not a number. ad/(bc) is valid only when c ≠ 0. 1/2 is rational (not needing to be an integer), and √2 is not rational, so it is not a valid input.","source":"2025 sample midterm Q6d","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m05-011","chapter":"m05","topic":"Rational numbers","type":"tf","q":"Every rational number is an integer.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"½ is rational but not an integer. The true direction is Theorem 4.3.1: every integer n is rational, since n = n/1.","source":"Epp Theorem 4.3.1, p. 185","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §13 Rational numbers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-012","chapter":"m05","topic":"Rational numbers","type":"tf","q":"The product of any two rational numbers is rational.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(a/b)(c/d) = ac/(bd); ac and bd are integers and bd ≠ 0 by the zero product property. (The quotient version fails only because of a zero divisor.)","source":"Epp §4.3, exercise set (closure of ℚ under ×), p. 187","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §13 Rational numbers","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-013","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"mcq","q":"\"There is an integer n > 3 such that 2ⁿ − 1 is prime.\" True or false?","options":["True: n = 4 gives 15","False: 2ⁿ − 1 is composite for all n > 3","False: 2ⁿ − 1 is even for n > 3","True: n = 5 gives 31"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"An existential statement needs just one witness: 2⁵ − 1 = 31 is prime (127 for n = 7 also works). 15 = 3·5 is composite, and 2ⁿ − 1 is always odd.","source":"2025 sample midterm Q6b","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m05-014","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"mcq","q":"\"For all integers a, b, c, if a, b, c are consecutive, then a + b + c is even.\" True or false?","options":["True: a + b + c = 3(a + 1)","False: 2 + 3 + 4 = 9","False: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6","True: among three consecutive integers one is even"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"One counterexample disproves ∀: 9 is odd. 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 is even, so it is not a counterexample; 3(a + 1) is even only when a is odd.","source":"2025 sample midterm Q6c","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m05-015","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"mcq","q":"Which pair (a, b) disproves\n∀ real a and b, if a² = b² then a = b ?","options":["a = 2, b = 2","a = 2, b = 4","a = −2, b = 2","a = −2, b = −2"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"A counterexample needs the hypothesis true and the conclusion false: (−2)² = 2² = 4 but −2 ≠ 2. In (2, 4) the hypothesis already fails, so it proves nothing.","source":"Epp Example 4.1.4, p. 165 · 2025 wk5 slide 19","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §5 Disproving universal statements by counterexample","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-016","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"mcq","q":"Which n is a counterexample to\n∀ integers n, if n is prime then (−1)ⁿ = −1 ?","options":["n = 2","n = 3","n = 1","n = 9"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"2 is prime but (−1)² = 1. For odd primes like 3 the claim holds; 1 and 9 are not prime, so the hypothesis is false and they cannot be counterexamples.","source":"Epp §4.2 exercise, 2025 wk5 slide 39","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §12 Exercises: true or false","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-017","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"tf","q":"If a sum of two integers is even, then one of the summands is even.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Counterexample: 1 + 3 = 4 is even, yet both summands are odd. Odd + odd is always even, so every pair of odd integers is a counterexample.","source":"Epp §4.2 exercise 24, Appendix B · 2025 wk5 slide 41","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §12 Exercises: true or false","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-018","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"mcq","q":"Which pair disproves\n∀ real x and y, ⌊x − y⌋ = ⌊x⌋ − ⌊y⌋ ?","options":["x = 2.5, y = 0.5","x = 3, y = 1","x = 2, y = 0.5","x = 1.5, y = 1"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"⌊2 − 0.5⌋ = ⌊1.5⌋ = 1 but ⌊2⌋ − ⌊0.5⌋ = 2 − 0 = 2. With x = 2.5, y = 0.5 both sides are 2; whenever y is an integer the equation holds.","source":"Epp ch. 4 intro, p. 160 · 2025 wk5 slide 9 (professor's ink)","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §2 New chapter: what a proof is","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-019","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"mcq","q":"r and s are integers. To prove \"∃ an integer k such that 22r + 18s = 2k\", which witness do you name?","options":["k = 11r + 9s","k = 22r + 18s","k = 11r + 18s","k = (22r + 18s)/4"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"2(11r + 9s) = 22r + 18s, and 11r + 9s is an integer because sums and products of integers are integers. (22r + 18s)/4 need not be an integer at all.","source":"Epp Example 4.1.3(b), p. 164 · 2025 wk5 slide 17","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §4 Proving existential statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-020","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"mcq","q":"Why is \"∃ a positive integer n such that n² + 3n + 2 is prime\" false?","options":["n² + 3n + 2 is always even, and no even number is prime","It fails for n = 1, 2, 3, so it fails for all n","n² + 3n + 2 is a perfect square","n² + 3n + 2 = (n + 1)(n + 2), a product of two integers > 1"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"To refute ∃ you prove the universal negation for an arbitrary n; the factorization does it. \"Always even\" is true but ignores that 2 is prime, and checking three cases is arguing from examples.","source":"Epp §4.2, 2025 wk5 slide 38","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §11 Showing that an existential statement is false","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-021","chapter":"m05","topic":"Existence and counterexample","type":"mcq","q":"When can the method of exhaustion prove \"∀x ∈ D, if P(x) then Q(x)\"?","options":["Whenever the statement has been checked on many examples","When D, or the set of x satisfying P(x), is finite and small enough to check","Whenever D is a subset of the integers","Never — exhaustion only proves existential statements"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Exhaustion checks every case, so it only works when the cases are finitely many (e.g. even n with 4 ≤ n ≤ 26 as sums of two primes). Many examples of an infinite domain is the mistake \"arguing from examples\".","source":"Epp Example 4.1.5, p. 166 · 2025 wk5 slide 21","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §6 Proving universal statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-022","chapter":"m05","topic":"Direct proof","type":"mcq","q":"Which sentence correctly starts a direct proof that the sum of any two even integers is even?","options":["Let m = 14 and n = 6, which are even integers.","Suppose m and n are any (particular but arbitrarily chosen) even integers.","Suppose m + n is even, where m and n are integers.","Suppose m = 2k and n = 2k for some integer k."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Direct proof supposes the hypothesis for generic elements. Specific values argue from examples, supposing m + n even assumes the conclusion, and using k twice forces m = n.","source":"Epp Method of Direct Proof, p. 167 · 2025 wk5 slides 24–26","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §7 The method of direct proof and Theorem 4.1.1","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-023","chapter":"m05","topic":"Direct proof","type":"mcq","q":"n = 2k + 1 for an integer k. Which line correctly finishes the algebra in the proof that 5n² + 7 is even?","options":["5n² + 7 = 2(10k² + 10k + 3) + 1","5n² + 7 = 2(5k² + 5k + 6)","5n² + 7 = 20k² + 20k + 8","5n² + 7 = 2(10k² + 10k + 6)"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"5(4k² + 4k + 1) + 7 = 20k² + 20k + 12 = 2(10k² + 10k + 6). Then name t = 10k² + 10k + 6, say t ∈ ℤ and invoke the definition of even — the board version stopped too early.","source":"Epp §4.1 exercise 31 · 2025 wk5 slide 30","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §8 Another example: 5n² + 7","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-024","chapter":"m05","topic":"Direct proof","type":"tf","q":"The difference of the squares of any two consecutive integers is odd.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(a + 1)² − a² = 2a + 1, twice an integer plus 1. The other order gives −(2a + 1) = 2(−a − 1) + 1, still odd.","source":"Epp §4.2 exercise 36 · 2025 wk5 slide 40","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §12 Exercises: true or false","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-025","chapter":"m05","topic":"Common mistakes","type":"mcq","q":"\"Suppose m and n are odd. Then m = 2k + 1 and n = 2k + 1 where k is an integer.\" Which mistake is this?","options":["Using the same letter to mean two different things","Jumping to a conclusion","Assuming what is to be proved","Arguing from examples"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Using k for both forces m = n, so the proof only covers identical integers. Existential instantiation needs a fresh name: m = 2k + 1, n = 2l + 1.","source":"Epp Common Mistakes 2, p. 176 · 2025 wk5 slide 32","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §10 Common mistakes","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-026","chapter":"m05","topic":"Common mistakes","type":"mcq","q":"\"m = 2r and n = 2s, so m + n = 2r + 2s. So m + n is even.\" Which mistake is this?","options":["Using the same letter to mean two different things","Misuse of the word if","Jumping to a conclusion","Confusing what is known with what is to be shown"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"It never shows m + n equals 2·(an integer): it must factor 2(r + s) and say r + s ∈ ℤ. The variables r and s are distinct, so it is not a same-letter error.","source":"Epp Common Mistakes 3, p. 177 · 2025 wk5 slide 33","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §10 Common mistakes","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-027","chapter":"m05","topic":"Common mistakes","type":"mcq","q":"\"By definition of odd, m = 2a + 1 for any integer a.\" What is wrong?","options":["It should say \"for all integers a\"","Nothing — \"any\" and \"some\" mean the same here","The variable must be called k, not a","It should say \"for some integer a\" — a is determined by m"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"a is forced to be (m − 1)/2, so it exists but is not arbitrary: use \"some\". \"Any\" claims every a works, which would make m equal to every odd number.","source":"Epp Common Mistakes 6, p. 178 · 2025 wk5 slide 35","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §10 Common mistakes","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-028","chapter":"m05","topic":"Common mistakes","type":"mcq","q":"\"Suppose m and n are odd. When any odd integers are multiplied, their product is odd. Hence mn is odd.\" Which mistake is this?","options":["Arguing from examples","Assuming what is to be proved","Jumping to a conclusion","Using any where some is meant"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The middle sentence is the theorem itself, so the \"proof\" is circular. Nothing here is a specific example, so it is not arguing from examples.","source":"Epp Common Mistakes 4, p. 177 · 2025 wk5 slide 33","study":"archive-2025/A05-methods-of-proof-1.md — §10 Common mistakes","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m05-029","chapter":"m05","topic":"Divisibility proofs","type":"mcq","q":"Proof: for all integers a, b, c, if a | b and a | c then a | (2b − 3c).\nAfter \"b = ak and c = al for some integers k, l\", which line completes the argument?","options":["2b − 3c = a(2k + 3l), and 2k + 3l ∈ ℤ","2b − 3c = 2a(k − 3l), and k − 3l ∈ ℤ","2b − 3c = a(2k − 3l), and 2k − 3l ∈ ℤ","2b − 3c = a(2k − 3k), and −k ∈ ℤ"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"2ak − 3al = a(2k − 3l), and 2k − 3l is an integer, so a | (2b − 3c) by definition. Option 4 reuses k for c, the same-letter mistake.","source":"2025 Quiz I Section 1, Q4 (official key)","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §1 Quiz I — Section 1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m05-030","chapter":"m05","topic":"Divisibility proofs","type":"mcq","q":"Her 30-point proof rubric awards 5 points for \"declaring the sets of the new variables\". In the a | b, a | c proof, that means writing …","options":["\"for some integers k and l\" (k, l ∈ ℤ) when introducing b = ak, c = al","\"Proof:\" at the start and \"∎\" at the end","\"a, b, c are particular but arbitrarily chosen\"","the definition d | n ⇔ n = dk for some k"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"New variables from existential instantiation must be typed: k, l ∈ ℤ. The start/end markers and the supposition are separate rubric items (5 points each).","source":"2025 Quiz I key — proof rubric","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §0 How she grades","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m01-001","chapter":"m01","topic":"Statements & variables","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is a statement (a sentence that is true or false, but not both)?","options":["2 + x = 1","Istanbul is the capital of Turkey.","She is a college student.","Find a number x with x² = 4."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"It has a definite truth value (false, Ankara is the capital), so it is a statement. \"2 + x = 1\" depends on x and \"she\" is unspecified, so neither has a fixed truth value; an instruction is not true or false at all.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 16 · Epp p. 38","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §3 Three kinds of mathematical statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-002","chapter":"m01","topic":"Statements & variables","type":"tf","q":"\"Every even integer greater than 2 is the sum of two primes\" (Goldbach's conjecture) is a statement, even though nobody knows whether it is true.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"A statement only needs to be true or false; an unknown truth value is still a definite one. Not knowing which is not the same as having no truth value.","source":"2025 Week 2 slide 8 · Epp p. 38","study":"archive-2025/A02-logic-compound-statements.md — §2 Statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-003","chapter":"m01","topic":"Statement types","type":"mcq","q":"\"If 378 is divisible by 18, then 378 is divisible by 6\" is which kind of statement?","options":["Universal","Existential","Conditional","Universal existential"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"It says that if one thing is true then another must be: a conditional. It does not claim a property for all elements of a set (universal) or that something exists (existential).","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 17 · Epp p. 2","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §3 Three kinds of mathematical statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-004","chapter":"m01","topic":"Statement types","type":"mcq","q":"\"Every real number has an additive inverse\" is best classified as:","options":["Existential universal","Universal conditional","Existential","Universal existential"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"First part universal (for every real r), second part existential (there is s with r + s = 0), and s depends on r. Existential universal would be \"there is one s that works for every r\".","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 20 · Epp p. 4","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §3 Universal existential statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-005","chapter":"m01","topic":"Statement types","type":"mcq","q":"Which is a correct formal rewrite of \"There is a positive integer that is less than or equal to every positive integer\"?","options":["There is a positive integer m such that for every positive integer n, m ≤ n.","For every positive integer n, there is a positive integer m such that m ≤ n.","For every positive integer m, if m is positive then m ≤ n.","There are positive integers m and n such that m ≤ n."],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The sentence is existential universal: one m (namely 1) works for all n, so ∃ comes first. Putting \"for every n\" first lets m depend on n, which is a weaker, different statement.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 21 · Epp p. 5","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §3 Existential universal statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-006","chapter":"m01","topic":"Statement types","type":"mcq","q":"Which sentence says the same as \"For every real number x, if x is nonzero then x² is positive\"?","options":["If the square of a real number is positive, then the number is nonzero.","All nonzero real numbers have positive squares.","There is a nonzero real number whose square is positive.","For every real number x, x² is positive."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"It is Epp's own rewrite of the universal conditional. The second option swaps hypothesis and conclusion (the converse), the third is merely existential, and the fourth drops the condition and is false at x = 0.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 18 · Epp p. 3 (Example 1.1.2)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §3 Universal conditional statements","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-007","chapter":"m01","topic":"Statement types","type":"tf","q":"\"For every real number r there is a real number s such that s > r\" and \"There is a real number s such that for every real number r, s > r\" say the same thing.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Order matters. The first is true (take s = r + 1, which depends on r). The second claims one s larger than every real, including s itself, so it is false.","source":"2026 Week 1 slides 19–21 · Epp pp. 4–5","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §3 (⚠️ Order matters)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-008","chapter":"m01","topic":"Sets & subsets","type":"mcq","q":"How many elements does the set {1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3} have?","options":["6","4","3","1"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"By the axiom of extension a set is determined by its elements; repetition does not matter, so it equals {1, 2, 3}. Counting the listed symbols (6) is the trap.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 23 · Epp p. 7 (Example 1.2.1)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §4 The language of sets","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-009","chapter":"m01","topic":"Sets & subsets","type":"mcq","q":"Let Uₙ = {n, −n}. How many elements does U₀ have?","options":["2","0","Undefined, since −0 is not a number","1"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"U₀ = {0, −0} = {0, 0} = {0}: one element. Writing a symbol twice does not create a second element.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 23 · Epp p. 7 (Example 1.2.1d)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §4 Example 1.2.1","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-010","chapter":"m01","topic":"Sets & subsets","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is true?","options":["{2} ⊆ {1, 2, 3}","{2} ∈ {1, 2, 3}","2 ⊆ {1, 2, 3}","{2} ⊆ {{1}, {2}}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Every element of {2} (just 2) is in {1, 2, 3}. The set {2} is not itself an element of {1, 2, 3}; 2 is a number, not a set, so \"2 ⊆\" is false; and 2 is not an element of {{1}, {2}}.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 26 · Epp p. 10 (Example 1.2.4)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §4 Example 1.2.4 — ∈ vs ⊆","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-011","chapter":"m01","topic":"Sets & subsets","type":"tf","q":"{2} ∈ {{1}, {2}}","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The elements of {{1}, {2}} are the sets {1} and {2}, so {2} is an element. (By contrast {2} ⊆ {{1}, {2}} is false, because the number 2 is not an element.)","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 26 · Epp p. 10 (Example 1.2.4f)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §4 Example 1.2.4 — ∈ vs ⊆","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-012","chapter":"m01","topic":"Sets & subsets","type":"mcq","q":"{x ∈ Z | −2 < x < 5} =","options":["{−2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}","{−1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4}","{1, 2, 3, 4}","the open interval (−2, 5)"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Integers strictly between −2 and 5. Including the endpoints ignores the strict <; {1, 2, 3, 4} is the answer for Z⁺; the interval is the answer for R.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 24 · Epp p. 8 (Example 1.2.2)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §4 Set-builder notation","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-013","chapter":"m01","topic":"Sets & subsets","type":"yn","q":"Let A = Z⁺ and B = {n ∈ Z | 0 ≤ n ≤ 100}. Is B ⊆ A?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"0 ∈ B but 0 is not a positive integer, so not every element of B is in A. One element is enough to break ⊆.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 26 · Epp p. 9 (Example 1.2.3)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §4 Subsets","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-014","chapter":"m01","topic":"Tuples & Cartesian products","type":"mcq","q":"Kuratowski's definition of the ordered pair (a, b) as a set is:","options":["{a, b}","{{a}, {b}}","{{a}, {a, b}}","{{b}, {a, b}}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"(a, b) := {{a}, {a, b}}; a lies in both inner sets, so the first coordinate can be recovered. {a, b} = {b, a} loses the order, {{a}, {b}} is symmetric in a and b too, and {{b}, {a, b}} is the pair (b, a).","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 27 · Epp p. 10","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §5 Cartesian products and strings","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-015","chapter":"m01","topic":"Tuples & Cartesian products","type":"mcq","q":"A = {x, y}, B = {1, 2, 3}, C = {a, b}. How many elements does A × B × C have?","options":["7","6","8","12"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"|A × B × C| = |A|·|B|·|C| = 2·3·2 = 12 ordered triples. Adding the sizes (7) is the usual slip; 6 is |A × B| only.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 30 · Epp p. 12 (Example 1.2.7)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §5 Example 1.2.7","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-016","chapter":"m01","topic":"Tuples & Cartesian products","type":"tf","q":"(A × B) × C and A × B × C are the same set.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"They have the same number of elements but different objects: elements of (A × B) × C are pairs like ((x, 1), a) whose first entry is a pair, elements of A × B × C are triples (x, 1, a).","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 30 · Epp p. 12 (Example 1.2.7)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §5 Example 1.2.7","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-017","chapter":"m01","topic":"Relations & functions","type":"mcq","q":"A = {1, 2}, B = {1, 2, 3}, and (x, y) ∈ R iff (x − y)/2 is an integer. Then R =","options":["{(1, 1), (1, 3), (2, 2)}","{(1, 2), (2, 1), (2, 3)}","{(1, 1), (2, 2)}","{(1, 1), (1, 3), (2, 2), (3, 1)}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"x − y must be even: (1,1) gives 0/2 = 0, (1,3) gives −2/2 = −1, (2,2) gives 0; the pairs (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) give ±1/2. The last option includes (3, 1), which is not even in A × B since 3 ∉ A.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 35 · Epp p. 16 (Example 1.3.1)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §6 Relations","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-018","chapter":"m01","topic":"Relations & functions","type":"mcq","q":"A = {2, 4, 6}, B = {1, 3, 5}, and S relates x to y iff y = x + 1. Why is S not a function from A to B?","options":["Two arrows leave the element 4.","6 has no image in B, because 7 ∉ B.","Two inputs are sent to the same output.","Some elements of B receive no arrow."],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Property 1 (every input has an output) fails at 6. Two inputs sharing an output and unused co-domain elements are both allowed in a function; two arrows out of 4 is the defect of R in the same example, not of S.","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 38 · Epp p. 19 (Example 1.3.4)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §7 Functions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-019","chapter":"m01","topic":"Relations & functions","type":"tf","q":"In a function, two different elements of the domain may be sent to the same element of the co-domain.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"A function needs exactly one arrow leaving each domain element; nothing forbids two arrows arriving at the same element (e.g. T(4) = T(6) = 1).","source":"2026 Week 1 slide 38 · Epp p. 19 (Example 1.3.4c)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §7 Functions","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-020","chapter":"m01","topic":"Graphs & pigeonhole","type":"mcq","q":"A vertex has two ordinary edges and one loop incident on it. What is its degree?","options":["3","2","4","5"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"deg(v) counts incident edges with a loop counted twice: 1 + 1 + 2 = 4. Counting the loop once gives 3.","source":"Epp p. 31 (Example 1.4.8)","study":"01-speaking-mathematically.md — §8 Graphs","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m01-021","chapter":"m01","topic":"Graphs & pigeonhole","type":"mcq","q":"In the pigeonhole principle f : X → Y, which condition guarantees two distinct x₁, x₂ ∈ X with f(x₁) = f(x₂)?","options":["|X| < |Y|","|X| = |Y|","|Y| > 0","|X| > |Y|"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"More pigeons (elements of X) than holes (elements of Y) forces a shared hole. With |X| ≤ |Y| a one-to-one assignment can exist, e.g. 7 people and 7 weekdays.","source":"2025 Week 1 slide 12 · 2026 Week 1 slide 12 · Epp Thm 9.4.1","study":"archive-2025/A01-speaking-mathematically-2025.md — §8 The pigeonhole principle","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-001","chapter":"m06","topic":"Divisibility","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement is true?","options":["0 | 5, because 5 = 0·k for some k","0 | 0, because 0 = 0·1","5 | 0, because 0 = 5·0","5 | 0 is false, because 0/5 is undefined"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"d | n needs n = dk for some integer k and d ≠ 0; 0 = 5·0 works. The divisor 0 is excluded by definition, so neither 0 | 5 nor 0 | 0 holds — and 0/5 = 0 is perfectly defined.","source":"Epp §4.4 definition, p. 190 · 2025 wk5 slide 6","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §2 Divisibility","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-002","chapter":"m06","topic":"Divisibility","type":"tf","q":"For all integers a and b, if a | b then a ≤ b.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Counterexample a = 3, b = 0: 3 | 0 but 3 > 0 (or b = −3). Theorem 4.4.1 needs a and b positive; only then does k ≥ 1 give b = ak ≥ a.","source":"Epp Theorem 4.4.1, p. 191 · 2025 wk5 slide 7","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §2 Divisibility","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-003","chapter":"m06","topic":"Divisibility","type":"mcq","q":"Proof that a | b and b | c imply a | c: from b = ak and c = bl (k, l ∈ ℤ), the key step is …","options":["c = (ak)l = a(kl), and kl ∈ ℤ","c = a + kl, and a + kl ∈ ℤ","a = c/(kl), so a is a factor of c","b = ak = c/l, so c = akl only when l = 1"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Substitute b = ak into c = bl and regroup by associativity: c = a(kl) with kl an integer, which is the definition of a | c. Writing a = c/(kl) uses division, which ℤ is not closed under.","source":"Epp Theorem 4.4.3, p. 193 · 2025 wk5 slide 8","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §2 Divisibility","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-004","chapter":"m06","topic":"Divisibility","type":"mcq","q":"Prove: if a | c and b | d then ab | cd. With c = ak and d = bl (k, l ∈ ℤ), which line finishes it?","options":["cd = ab(k + l), and k + l ∈ ℤ","cd = (a + b)(kl)","cd = abk, and k ∈ ℤ","cd = ab(kl), and kl ∈ ℤ"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"cd = (ak)(bl) = ab(kl) by commutativity and associativity; kl is an integer, so ab | cd. Adding k + l would be the pattern for sums, not products.","source":"2025 Quiz I Section 2, Q4 (official key)","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §2 Quiz I — Section 2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m06-005","chapter":"m06","topic":"Divisibility","type":"tf","q":"For all integers a, b, c, if a | b then a | bc.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"b = ak gives bc = a(kc) with kc ∈ ℤ, so a | bc. The converse-style claim \"a | bc ⇒ a | b or a | c\" is false (6 | 2·3 but 6 ∤ 2, 6 ∤ 3).","source":"Epp §4.4 exercises, p. 197; checked for |a|, |b|, |c| ≤ 15","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §2 Divisibility","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-006","chapter":"m06","topic":"Quotient-remainder, div & mod","type":"mcq","q":"By the quotient-remainder theorem, −54 = 4q + r with 0 ≤ r < 4. What are q and r?","options":["q = −13, r = −2","q = −14, r = 2","q = −13, r = 2","q = −14, r = −2"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"4·(−14) + 2 = −56 + 2 = −54 with 0 ≤ 2 < 4. q = −13, r = −2 also gives −54 but violates r ≥ 0: the quotient rounds down, not toward 0.","source":"Epp Example 4.5.1, p. 201","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §4 The Quotient-Remainder Theorem, div and mod","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-007","chapter":"m06","topic":"Quotient-remainder, div & mod","type":"mcq","q":"Compute 32 div 9 and 32 mod 9.","options":["32 div 9 = 5, 32 mod 9 = 3","32 div 9 = 3, 32 mod 9 = 5","32 div 9 = 3, 32 mod 9 = 6","32 div 9 = 4, 32 mod 9 = −4"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"32 = 9·3 + 5 with 0 ≤ 5 < 9: div is the quotient 3, mod the remainder 5. A negative \"remainder\" is never allowed.","source":"Epp Example 4.5.2, p. 201","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §4 The Quotient-Remainder Theorem, div and mod","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-008","chapter":"m06","topic":"Quotient-remainder, div & mod","type":"mcq","q":"Compute 54 div 70 and 54 mod 70.","options":["1 and −16","70 and 54","0 and 16","0 and 54"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"54 = 70·0 + 54 and 0 ≤ 54 < 70. When n is smaller than a positive d, the quotient is 0 and the remainder is n itself.","source":"Epp Example 4.5.1(c), p. 201","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §4 The Quotient-Remainder Theorem, div and mod","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-009","chapter":"m06","topic":"Quotient-remainder, div & mod","type":"mcq","q":"m is an integer with m mod 11 = 6. What is 4m mod 11?","options":["2","24","6","13"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"m = 11q + 6, so 4m = 44q + 24 = 11(4q + 2) + 2, and 0 ≤ 2 < 11. 24 is not a valid remainder because it exceeds 10.","source":"Epp Example 4.5.4(b), p. 202","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §4 The Quotient-Remainder Theorem, div and mod","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-010","chapter":"m06","topic":"Quotient-remainder, div & mod","type":"mcq","q":"Which remainders can n² + 5 leave on division by 4, as n ranges over ℤ?","options":["1 only","0 and 1 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"n = 2k gives 4(k² + 1) + 1; n = 2k + 1 gives 4(k² + k + 1) + 2. Remainder 0 never occurs, which proves 4 ∤ n² + 5 (her midterm bonus).","source":"2025 sample midterm Bonus","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m06-011","chapter":"m06","topic":"Quotient-remainder, div & mod","type":"mcq","q":"Today is Tuesday. What day of the week is it 365 days from now (no leap day in between)?","options":["Tuesday","Monday","Thursday","Wednesday"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"365 = 7·52 + 1, so 365 mod 7 = 1 and the weekday moves forward by one. Answering Tuesday would assume 365 is a multiple of 7.","source":"Epp Example 4.5.3, p. 202","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §4 The Quotient-Remainder Theorem, div and mod","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-012","chapter":"m06","topic":"Floor and ceiling","type":"mcq","q":"What are ⌊−2.7⌋ and ⌈−2.7⌉?","options":["⌊−2.7⌋ = −2, ⌈−2.7⌉ = −3","⌊−2.7⌋ = −3, ⌈−2.7⌉ = −2","⌊−2.7⌋ = −2, ⌈−2.7⌉ = −2","⌊−2.7⌋ = −3, ⌈−2.7⌉ = −3"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Floor is the greatest integer ≤ x: −3 ≤ −2.7 < −2. Ceiling is the least integer ≥ x: −2. Floor rounds toward −∞, not toward 0.","source":"Epp §4.6 definitions, p. 212 · 2025 wk5 slides 16–17","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §5 Floor and ceiling","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-013","chapter":"m06","topic":"Floor and ceiling","type":"mcq","q":"Which pair disproves\n∀ real x and y, ⌊x + y⌋ = ⌊x⌋ + ⌊y⌋ ?","options":["x = 1.2, y = 2","x = 0.3, y = 0.4","x = y = ½","x = 1.5, y = 0.4"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"⌊½ + ½⌋ = ⌊1⌋ = 1 but ⌊½⌋ + ⌊½⌋ = 0. The fractional parts must add to at least 1; with y = 2 (an integer) Theorem 4.6.1 guarantees equality.","source":"Epp Example 4.6.4, p. 213","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §5 Floor and ceiling","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-014","chapter":"m06","topic":"Floor and ceiling","type":"tf","q":"⌊x + m⌋ = ⌊x⌋ + m for every real number x and every REAL number m.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Theorem 4.6.1 needs m to be an integer. With x = m = ½: ⌊1⌋ = 1 but ⌊½⌋ + ½ = ½.","source":"Epp Theorem 4.6.1, p. 214 · 2025 wk5 slide 18","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §5 Floor and ceiling","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-015","chapter":"m06","topic":"Floor and ceiling","type":"mcq","q":"For an odd integer n, ⌊n/2⌋ equals …","options":["(n − 1)/2","(n + 1)/2","n/2","(n − 2)/2"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"n = 2k + 1 gives ⌊k + ½⌋ = k = (n − 1)/2 (also for negative n, e.g. ⌊−3/2⌋ = −2). (n + 1)/2 is the ceiling instead.","source":"Epp Theorem 4.6.2, p. 215","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §5 Floor and ceiling","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-016","chapter":"m06","topic":"Proof by cases","type":"mcq","q":"Prove: if m and n have the same parity, then 5m + 7n is even.\nIn the case m = 2k + 1, n = 2l + 1, which line is correct?","options":["5m + 7n = 2(5k + 7l) + 12, which is odd","5m + 7n = 2(5k + 7l + 6) + 1","5m + 7n = 2(5k + 7l + 6)","5m + 7n = 2(5k + 7l + 12)"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"10k + 5 + 14l + 7 = 10k + 14l + 12 = 2(5k + 7l + 6), twice an integer. You need both cases (both even, both odd) for a complete division-into-cases proof.","source":"2025 sample quiz Q4","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §3 Sample Quiz","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m06-017","chapter":"m06","topic":"Proof by cases","type":"mcq","q":"Prove: any two consecutive integers m and m + 1 have opposite parity.\nIn Case 2 (m = 2k + 1), what shows m + 1 is even?","options":["m + 1 = 2k + 2 = 2(k + 1), and k + 1 ∈ ℤ","m + 1 = 2k + 1 + 1, which is odd plus one","m + 1 = 2(k + 1) + 1","m + 1 = 2k, since the 1s cancel"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(2k + 1) + 1 = 2(k + 1) with k + 1 an integer, so m + 1 is even by definition. Case 1 (m = 2k) gives m + 1 = 2k + 1, odd.","source":"2025 sample midterm Q9 · Epp Theorem 4.5.2, pp. 203–204","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §3 Parity and proof by division into cases","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m06-018","chapter":"m06","topic":"Proof by cases","type":"mcq","q":"Proof by division into cases rests on which valid argument form?","options":["p → q, q ∴ p","p → q, ¬p ∴ ¬q","p ∨ q, p ∴ ¬q","p ∨ q, p → r, q → r ∴ r"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"If C follows from each of A₁, …, Aₙ and one of them must hold, C holds. The other three are invalid: converse error, inverse error, and \"or\" is inclusive.","source":"Epp §4.5 Method of Proof by Division into Cases, p. 204","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §3 Parity and proof by division into cases","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-019","chapter":"m06","topic":"Contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"Prove there is no least positive rational number. Suppose r is the least one. Which number gives the contradiction?","options":["r − 1","r/2","2r","1/r"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"r/2 is rational, positive and smaller than r — contradicting minimality. r − 1 may be negative, 2r is larger, and 1/r is smaller only when r > 1.","source":"2025 sample midterm Q7","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m06-020","chapter":"m06","topic":"Contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"Prove √(r + s) ≠ √r + √s for all positive reals r, s. Suppose equality holds and square both sides. What contradiction follows?","options":["r + s = r² + s², so r = s = 1","2√(rs) = 0, so r = 0 or s = 0","√(rs) = r + s, which is impossible","r + s = 0, so r = −s"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"r + s = r + 2√(rs) + s forces 2√(rs) = 0, hence rs = 0, so one of r, s is 0 — contradicting positivity. Squaring is legitimate here because both sides are nonnegative.","source":"2025 sample midterm Q8","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §4 Sample Midterm","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m06-021","chapter":"m06","topic":"Contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"Why do no integers m, n satisfy 12m + 3n = 2?","options":["12m + 3n is always even, but 2 is even too","m and n would have to be fractions, by trying small values","12m + 3n is always negative","12m + 3n = 3(4m + n), so 3 would divide 2"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"By contradiction: if it held, 2 = 3·(an integer), so 3 | 2, false since 2 = 3·0 + 2. Trying small values is arguing from examples.","source":"2025 question bank 1h","study":"archive-2025/AX-assessments-2025.md — §5 Questions from Quizzes, Midterm and Final","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m06-022","chapter":"m06","topic":"Contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"To prove \"the sum of any rational number and any irrational number is irrational\" by contradiction, you suppose …","options":["there exist a rational a and an irrational b such that a + b is rational","the sum of any rational and any irrational is rational","a is irrational, b is rational and a + b is irrational","for all rational a and irrational b, a + b is irrational"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The negation of ∀(P → Q) is ∃(P ∧ ¬Q). Option 2 keeps ∀ — Epp's flagged mistake. Option 4 is the statement itself, not its negation.","source":"Epp Theorem 4.7.3, p. 221 · 2025 wk5 slide 25","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §6 Indirect argument: proof by contradiction","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-023","chapter":"m06","topic":"Contraposition","type":"mcq","q":"To prove \"∀ integers n, if 5 ∤ n² then 5 ∤ n\" by contraposition, you prove …","options":["∀ integers n, if 5 | n² then 5 | n","∀ integers n, if 5 ∤ n then 5 ∤ n²","∀ integers n, if 5 | n then 5 | n²","∃ an integer n such that 5 ∤ n² and 5 | n"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Contrapositive of P → Q is ¬Q → ¬P: suppose 5 | n, so n = 5k and n² = 5(5k²). Option 2 is the converse, option 3 the inverse, option 4 the negation (used in contradiction).","source":"2025 question bank 1g · Epp §4.7 exercise 20 · 2025 wk5 slide 30","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §7 Indirect argument: proof by contraposition","origin":"professor"},{"id":"m06-024","chapter":"m06","topic":"Contraposition","type":"mcq","q":"Proving \"∀ integers n, if n² is even then n is even\" by contraposition: what do you suppose and what do you show?","options":["Suppose n² is even; show n is even","Suppose n is even; show n² is even","Suppose n² is even and n is odd; derive a contradiction","Suppose n is odd; show n² is odd"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"¬Q is \"n is not even\", i.e. odd (parity property), and ¬P is \"n² is odd\": (2k + 1)² = 2(2k² + 2k) + 1. Option 2 is a direct proof and option 4 is contradiction.","source":"Epp Proposition 4.7.4, p. 222 · 2025 wk5 slide 29","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §7 Indirect argument: proof by contraposition","origin":"practice"},{"id":"m06-025","chapter":"m06","topic":"Contraposition","type":"tf","q":"Every proof by contraposition can be rewritten as a proof by contradiction.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Suppose ∃x with P(x) and ¬Q(x); the contraposition steps then give ¬P(x), contradicting P(x). The reverse fails: \"√2 is irrational\" is not a universal conditional, so only contradiction applies.","source":"Epp §4.7 Relation between Proof by Contradiction and Proof by Contraposition, p. 223","study":"archive-2025/A06-methods-of-proof-2.md — §7 Indirect argument: proof by contraposition","origin":"practice"}]};
